top: THEORY
%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
% pvs_cad: a formally verified, executable computer algebra core for PVS,
% centered on cylindrical algebraic decomposition.  See ../CAD_PLAN.md.
%
% Phase 0 (baseline and harness)
%   eval_probe   throwaway experiment: recursive multivariate polynomial
%                datatype under the ground evaluator (design decision D1).
%                Not imported here: it defines meval, as_list, deg and the
%                arithmetic again over the same datatype, which would make
%                those names ambiguous in a theory that imports top
%
% Phase 1 (multivariate polynomial kernel)
%   mpoly        DATATYPE: mconst(rat) | mpol(list[mpoly]) (imported through
%                mpoly_def, not directly)
%   mpoly_def    size measure, positional evaluation (head of the valuation
%                is the main variable), variables mvar(j), degree
%   mpoly_arith  madd, mscal, mneg, msub, mmulx (same-level and coefficient
%                mode), mmul, mpow; evaluation is a homomorphism for each
%   mpoly_norm   canonical form mnorm (no trailing zeros, constants collapsed),
%                meval_mnorm (soundness), normal?/normalx and the facts that
%                mnorm lands in normal? and is the identity on it
%   PolyExpr     DATATYPE: deep embedding of polynomial expressions (imported
%                through mpoly_embed, not directly)
%   mpoly_embed  peval, its rewrite lemmas, pto/pnorm, the reflection theorem
%                poly_eq_by_norm used by the mpoly-eq strategy
%   mpoly_examples  thirteen identities closed by (mpoly-eq), incl. a function
%                atom f(x), sq, division by a constant, and three through
%                unfolded user definitions
%   pvs-strategies  mpoly-eq (Lisp printer expression -> PolyExpr, reflection)
%                and mpoly-simp (normal form printed back and replaced in place)
%   mpoly_showcase  what the strategies can do: classical identities up to
%                8 variables, Chebyshev/Legendre definitions, 2x2 matrix
%                identities, opaque atoms, inequalities via identities,
%                in-place normalization
%   mpoly_coefs  bridge to reals@polynomials: coefficient sequences of the
%                head variable, meval_mpol_poly, poly_coefs_eq (equal
%                functions of the head variable have equal coefficients)
%   mpoly_unique uniqueness of normal forms: normal terms denoting the same
%                function are equal; mnorm(p) = mnorm(q) IFF eq_fun?(p, q)
%                (completeness of mpoly-eq)
%   mpoly_univ   the univariate view in the main variable: coef, lc, cf,
%                meval_coef; mderiv with meval_mderiv and mderiv_is_deriv
%                (analytic derivative); partial_eval to a Sturm Polylist
%                with partial_eval_polylist
%   mpoly_pdiv   pseudo-division in the main variable on coefficient lists:
%                lscal, shiftl, lbut/llast, lsub with length and evaluation
%                lemmas; pstep; pquo_rem with pquo_rem_eval
%                (b^pk * f = quo * g + rem) and pquo_rem_rem_length
%   mpoly_mono   conversion tm to NASALib mult_poly's monomial lists (shift0,
%                shift_up, bump on exponent vectors) with tm_eval:
%                full_eval(tm(p))(xs) = meval(p)(xs) when length(xs) >=
%                max_length(tm(p)); evaluation lemmas for
%                mult_poly's full_eval (nil, cons, append, scale, shifts)
%   mpoly_mono_examples  the bridge tm_peval (any PolyExpr reaches mult_poly
%                with its value preserved) and ground conversions checked by
%                evaluation
%
% Phase 2: real algebraic numbers on top of NASALib's Sturm library
%   alg_count    root counting on closed rational intervals: nroots_a on
%                integer arrays (roots_closed_int with its chain) and nroots
%                on Sturm Polylists via ipoly (clear denominators), with the
%                specifications nroots = 0 / >= 1 / = 1
%   alg_def      algebraic numbers as isolating intervals [# p, lb, ub #]
%                with the executable invariant alg?, value(a) (the unique
%                root), rational numbers as algebraic numbers, bisection
%                refine/refine_n keeping the value (refine decides the half by
%                a zero or sign change at the ends first, lhalf_def, and counts
%                roots only when that fails, 2026-10-01)
%   alg_rat_def  a rational value for an algebraic number when it has one
%                (executable, 2026-09-30): ratx tries the root of a linear
%                polynomial, then rationals with small denominators near the
%                middle of the refined interval; the n-level engine's samples
%                use it (towern_od's odS and osec), so a rational root is an
%                exact rational point and signs there are evaluations
%   alg_rat      the value it finds is the number's value (ratx_r, ratx_b)
%   alg_sortc_def  SORTING ALGEBRAIC NUMBERS WITH EACH STURM CHAIN COMPUTED
%                ONCE (executable, 2026-10-01): an algebraic number travels with
%                the chain of its polynomial (CA), refc bisects with it, cmpc
%                compares (disjoint intervals, bisection, equality by the
%                common roots of the two polynomials, i.e. their gcd); sortf is
%                cell1's insertion sort, sortsep also separates neighbours
%                (sepall), and sepcc is sect_inv's separation by bisection;
%                cell1's sortu and sect_inv's sepf evaluate them
%   alg_sortc    they are right: cmpc = sign3(value - value) (cmpc_def, eqg_def
%                by gcd_eq), separating keeps the values (sepcc_val,
%                sepall_vals)
%   alg_zero     q(value(a)) = 0 decided by counting the roots of p^2 + q^2
%                (integer arrays isq/sos with explicit degree and positive
%                leading coefficient); zero_at_def
%   alg_sign     sign of q at value(a): zero test, constant case, bisection
%                loop sep until q has no root in the interval (measure
%                real_ord_ep(qdist) with qdist = min_poly_root_dist of p*q),
%                then the Sturm library's compute_poly_sat decides the sign;
%                alg_sign_def
%   alg_isolate  all real roots of p as an increasing list of algebraic
%                numbers: half-open counting nroots_h (no double counting at
%                split points), shrink for a rational root, bisection iso
%                (measure induction with the root separation), Cauchy-type
%                bound rbound; iso_spec, roots_alg/complete/increasing
%   alg_order    equality and order of two algebraic numbers with different
%                polynomials: alg_eq by alg_sign, sepr bisects both until the
%                intervals are disjoint; alg_cmp_def, alg_lt_def
%   thom_lemma   Thom's lemma on coefficient sequences: iterated derivatives
%                dk, sign conditions sat, convexity of their realizations
%                (sat_convex, downward induction with NASALib's strict
%                monotonicity), thom_distinct (roots with the same derivative
%                signs coincide)
%   thom_enc     executable Thom encodings: pderiv/nderiv on Polylists,
%                thom(p, a) = signs of p^(d), ..., p' at value(a) by alg_sign,
%                nderiv_eval bridge, thom_encoding_distinct
%   alg_strategy support for the alg-roots strategy: upoly (Polylist of a
%                univariate PolyExpr), root_interval, sign_at_root
%   alg_strategy_examples  what alg-roots proves: root intervals and signs
%                of other polynomials at the roots, added as proved facts
%   alg_examples ground examples checked by eval-formula: root counts, the
%                representation of sqrt(2), its refinement, signs, isolation
%                of all roots (up to a quintic), comparisons, Thom encodings
%
% Phase 3: branching quantifier elimination
%   btree / branch_tree  sign-condition branch trees: a node branches on the
%                sign of an mpoly in the parameters, a leaf carries a result;
%                select follows the signs at a valuation, branches lists the
%                leaves with their path conditions, and branch_exists and
%                branch_sound make that list an exhaustive and unambiguous
%                case analysis of the parameters (imported through
%                branch_map, branch_norm and others -- btree through
%                branch_tree -- not directly)
%   branch_map   bmap and bbind, so one symbolic step followed by another is
%                again a branch tree; select commutes with both
%   branch_norm  the symbolic degree: snorm branches on the sign of the
%                leading coefficient until it is known nonzero, preserving the
%                polynomial as a function (snorm_eval, snorm_lc)
%   poly_unique  uniqueness of division with remainder over the reals, in the
%                plain and the scaled form, which NASALib's poly_divide does
%                not provide
%   mpoly_real   earr, the evaluation homomorphism from a coefficient list
%                over mpoly to a real coefficient array, the form every
%                theorem of NASALib's Sturm development is stated in
%   prem_arr     the symbolic pseudo-remainder of Phase 1 is exactly b^k
%                times the remainder of NASALib's poly_divide, one valuation
%                at a time
%   mpoly_prod   lmul, the product of two coefficient lists, and earr_lmul:
%                the array it denotes is NASALib's polynomial_prod of the
%                arrays of the factors, proved by coefficient uniqueness
%                rather than by computing the convolution
%   snorm_arr    normalizing leaves the array alone, not only the polynomial
%                (snorm_earr), so the degree of the normalized list is the top
%                index at which the array is nonzero (snorm_dg_eq)
%   earr_deriv   the formal derivative of a coefficient list, and the array it
%                denotes is NASALib's poly_deriv of the array of the list
%   branch_prs   the pseudo-remainder sequence as a branch tree: prs iterates
%                the step and the degree branching, rchain is the same
%                recursion at one valuation, select_prs identifies them, and
%                the rchain lemmas are the structure of the sequence (it ends
%                with the zero polynomial, the earlier elements have nonzero
%                leading coefficients, the lengths strictly decrease, and each
%                element is the normalized step of the two before it)
%   tarski_chain the chain of a Tarski query: the same recursion with g times
%                the derivative as its second element, and tchain_sturm, which
%                is Tarski@sturmtarski's constructed_sturm_sequence? of it at
%                every valuation; gchain and its lemmas are the chain
%                structure with an arbitrary second element
%   tarski_count the Tarski query of a symbolic pair at a valuation: tq_val
%                says it is #{roots where g > 0} - #{roots where g < 0}
%   tarski_dec   two queries decide a sign condition at the roots: twice the
%                number of roots where q is positive is TQ(p, q) + TQ(p, q^2),
%                so sign_exists decides EXISTS x: p(x) = 0 AND q(x) > 0
%   branch_sgn   the sequence with the sign of every leading coefficient in
%                the leaf (snormp, prsp), so the counts below need no further
%                branching
%   sgn_count    the number of sign changes of the chain at plus and at minus
%                infinity, computed from the signs the leaf carries
%   root_dec     qdec, a tree of sign conditions whose leaves are TRUE or
%                FALSE, and qdec_sound: the leaf the parameters select answers
%                EXISTS x: p(x, ys) = 0
%   pos_dec      pdec and pdec_sound: the same tree decides whether a
%                symbolic polynomial is everywhere positive, since a
%                polynomial with no root keeps the sign of its leading
%                coefficient
%   crit_pos     the analytic step the sign conditions need: a polynomial of
%                even degree with a negative leading coefficient is positive
%                somewhere exactly when it is positive at a critical point
%                (crit_exists), from the extreme value theorem and the
%                vanishing of the derivative at an interior maximum
%   mpoly_cst    mcst? recognizes a term that denotes a constant and cst_val
%                is that constant (mcst_eval)
%   btree_prune  prune replaces every node whose polynomial is a constant by
%                the child that constant selects, and select_prune says the
%                branch the parameters take is unchanged, so the soundness of
%                a decision carries over to the pruned tree
%   sgn_prune    sdecide, the sign-condition tree pruned: for x^2 - 1 and x it
%                has one branch instead of 48827, and psign_branch /
%                pnosign_branch are what the strategies use
%   sgn_examples the poly-sign and poly-nosign strategies: a root of x^2 - 1
%                where x is positive, none of x^2 + 1, and the parametric
%                statements about x - a and x^2 - a; g6 needs poly-nosign's
%                branch facts (no positive root of x^2 + a for a > 0)
%   tarski_pair  two sign conditions at the roots of one polynomial: the four
%                queries of the pairs q1^a q2^b with a, b in {1, 2} count the
%                roots where both are positive four times over (pair_count),
%                so pair_exists decides EXISTS x: p = 0 AND q1 > 0 AND q2 > 0;
%                NASALib has this for families but only over integer
%                coefficient arrays, and the symbolic chains produce real ones
%   tarski_two   the symbolic side of the pair: the four query polynomials as
%                normalized products of coefficient lists (lmn and its six
%                lemmas), their arrays and degrees, and two_exists, which
%                decides EXISTS x: p = 0 AND q1 > 0 AND q2 > 0 from four of
%                the chains of tarski_chain
%   tarski_multi the restricted Tarski query T(p, P, f) --- the query of f over
%                the roots of p at which every function of P is positive ---
%                and T_step, which halves one condition into two queries, so
%                m conditions cost 2^m; T_exists decides the conjunction
%   tarski_many  the same on coefficient lists: mts is the executable
%                recursion (2^m chains) and many_exists decides
%                EXISTS x: p = 0 AND every q in Q: q > 0
%   poly_farinf  a list of polynomials far out on either side: all leading
%                coefficients positive, or all positive after (-1)^deg, and
%                both converses
%   poly_rolle   the conjunction with no equation: it holds somewhere exactly
%                when it holds far right, far left, or at a critical point of
%                the product (ineq_exists), by the sup and inf of the roots of
%                the product on each side and Rolle's theorem
%   qe_conj      one conjunct: several equations collapse into one by a sum of
%                squares (sqsum_zero), and cdec_sound decides
%                EXISTS x: all equations and all strict inequalities, using
%                many_exists when there is an equation and ineq_exists when
%                there is none
%   qe_formula   a formula as a disjunction of such conjuncts, and qe_sound:
%                qe_exists decides EXISTS x: feval(F, ys, x) whenever the fuel
%                and the degrees are right.  This is quantifier elimination
%                for one variable; the Boolean normalization is done by the
%                strategy, outside the verified core, and every fact it
%                produces is proved
%   qe_run       the qe-exists strategy at work: a root of x^2 - 1 with x > 0,
%                none of x^2 + 1, a Boolean combination, two conjunctions with
%                no equation, a universally quantified statement, and the
%                paper's conflict-detection question with numbers, decided on
%                both sides
%   tarski_tree  the many-condition recursion as a branch tree: mtree
%                replaces every normalization of mts by a degree branching,
%                so its leaves are the counts, and select_mtree says the leaf
%                the parameters select is mts, with no hypothesis
%   many_ok      when the recursion of tarski_many is well formed: mok_at
%                reduces mok to nonvanishing leading coefficients and the
%                syntactic fuel length(p) + length(g) + mlen(Q); normalizing
%                the conditions keeps their values and does not lengthen them
%   many_tree    a root at which many conditions hold, on a tree: snormps
%                normalizes every condition with its sign, mcount asks
%                whether the count of tarski_tree is positive, and mcore
%                answers FALSE for a condition that is the zero polynomial;
%                mcore_sound needs the degree of p and the syntactic fuel
%                length(p) + 1 + mlen(Q) alone
%   ineq_ok      the no-equation conjunction at a valuation in the tree's
%                terms: the far-out conditions from the recorded signs
%                (posinfp_at, neginfp_at), the degree of the product as the
%                sum of the degrees, and the three cases of the Rolle
%                reduction (a constant product, a linear one with no critical
%                point, and the rest) with the fuel bound dgs_le
%   ineq_tree    itree decides EXISTS x: every q in Q: q(x, ys) > 0 on a
%                tree: normalize the conditions, answer TRUE far out, form
%                the product as a tree (wtree), FALSE for a product of length
%                at most two, and mcount at the derivative otherwise;
%                itree_sound needs the fuel 2 mlen(Q) + 1 alone
%   conj_tree    ctree decides one conjunct: the equations collapse to the
%                sum of squares, whose degree branching sends the zero case
%                to itree, the constant case to FALSE and the rest to mcore;
%                ctree_sound needs length(sqsum(E)) + 2 mlen(Q) + 1 <= k
%   qe_tree      qtree decides EXISTS x: feval(F, ys, x) for a formula of
%                qe_formula as a tree of sign conditions on the parameters
%                (qtree_sound, fuel tfuel(F)); qdecide is the pruned tree
%                (the parametric qe-exists strategy uses qe_ctx's qdecidec and
%                qe_branchc)
%   sign_oracle  deciding the sign of a coefficient under the conditions on
%                its path: with one parameter the question is one-variable QE
%                on the parameter (qe_exists of qe_formula with no parameters
%                left), forced(c, cx) is that decision or 2 for unknown, and
%                forced_sound says a decided sign is the sign at every
%                valuation the conditions admit
%   hutch_oracle the same question decided by NASALib's hutch, a sign-
%                invariant interval decomposition on integer chains with
%                content stripping: forced2, forced2_sound; this is the
%                oracle snormc uses, since forced took minutes per node once
%                the path held a few conditions of degree ten and more
%   branch_ctx   bbindc: bbind that passes the leaf's path to the
%                continuation; select_bbindc is its one select fact
%   snorm_ctx    snormc: the degree branching that asks sign_oracle before it
%                branches, and gives the zero child its new condition
%   prsp_ctx     prspc and ctreec: the chains built with snormc and bbindc,
%                with select_prspc and select_ctreec under the path
%   tarski_ctx   mtreec, snormpsc, mcountc, mcorec: the many-condition
%                recursion and the root decision with contexts
%   ineq_ctx     wtreec, itreec: the no-equation conjunction with contexts
%   qe_ctx       cjtreec, qtreec: one conjunct and a formula with contexts;
%                qdecidec is the tree from the empty path and qe_branchc is
%                what qe-exists uses: for x - a the tree has three branches
%                where qdecide had twenty-five
%   qe_symbolic  the parametric qe-exists at work: x - a, x^2 - a, a
%                parametric discriminant, and the conflict question with the
%                radius D symbolic
%   sgn_dec      gdec and gdec_sound: the sign condition on a tree with no
%                hypothesis on the valuation, the cases tok2 rules out
%                answered by exists_dec or by hand, so the fuel bound is the
%                only condition; sign_branch and nosign_branch (poly-sign and
%                poly-nosign use sgn_prune's sdecide with psign_branch and
%                pnosign_branch)
%   exists_dec   edec and edec_sound: the tree for EXISTS x: q(x, ys) > 0, a
%                case analysis on the normalized query whose last case is
%                crit_pos's and is answered by sdec for the derivative
%   sign_tree    sdec, a tree of sign conditions whose leaves are TRUE or
%                FALSE, and sdec_sound: the leaf the parameters select answers
%                EXISTS x: p(x, ys) = 0 AND q(x, ys) > 0, from the two
%                queries of tarski_dec read off the two chains
%   tarski_examples  the queries run: for x^2 - 1 and x the two queries are 0
%                and 2, so a root with x > 0 exists and the statement about
%                the reals is proved; for -x^2 the sum is zero and none does
%   pos_examples the poly-pos and poly-nonzero strategies: a positivity statement whose
%                polynomial has free parameters is proved by evaluating the
%                tree and discharging the branch conditions by arithmetic
%   qe_examples  the decision run by the ground evaluator on x^2 + y, on
%                a*x + b (where the degree branching decides) and on the
%                quadratic, and a fact about the reals proved by running it
%
% Phase 4: subresultants
%   ring_det     the Laplace determinant of a matrix of polynomials in the
%                parameters (detl, executable) and of a real matrix, and
%                det_eval: evaluating the determinant is the determinant of
%                the evaluated matrix.  NASALib's det is built from
%                elementary matrices and has no expansion, so this is its own
%   sylvester    the Sylvester matrix of two coefficient lists, the resultant
%                res and the discriminant disc, and their specializations
%                res_eval and disc_eval
%
% Phase 5: cylindrical algebraic decomposition
%   cpoly_unique complex polynomials: n + 1 distinct roots make the zero
%                function (roots_bound), the zero function has zero
%                coefficients (coefs_unique, coefs_eq), the coefficients of
%                (x - y) q (shift_poly) and the leading coefficient of the
%                cofactor of a linear factor (factor_lc); NASALib's
%                fundamental_algebra has one root and one factor only
%   croots       cfact: a proper polynomial is its leading coefficient times
%                the product of n linear factors, the fundamental theorem of
%                algebra iterated
%   croot_near   root proximity: |p(z)| is bounded by (n+1) M B^n on |z| <= B,
%                every root of a proper polynomial lies in |z| <= 1 + n M/|a_n|
%                (root_bound), and for every eps there is a delta such that a
%                root of any polynomial whose coefficients are within delta of
%                p's is within eps of some root of p (croot_root_near) -- the
%                analytic input to delineability
%   sign_vec     sign vectors of a family (svec) and positions in a list
%   tree_sel     selecting a branch tree by a sign vector instead of a
%                valuation (sel_by, sel_by_sel), the conditions of a tree
%   bbindc_ex    the existential form of select_bbindc
%   rsc_tree     realized sign vectors at the roots of one polynomial with
%                side conditions, the adaptive enumeration on cjtreec
%                (rsc_sound, rsc_complete)
%   far_sign     the sign vectors at +oo and -oo from the normalized
%                leading coefficients (far_vec_right, far_vec_left)
%   sector_rep   the intermediate value theorem for a polynomial in the top
%                variable, the product of a family, sign vectors constant
%                between roots, Rolle, and consecutive roots (sector_cases)
%   svs_tree     every sign vector a family realizes along the top variable,
%                as a branch tree over the parameters: roots of the product,
%                roots of its derivative, the two ends (svs_sound,
%                svs_complete)
%   bform        Boolean formulas over sign atoms, evaluated at a point or
%                on a sign vector
%   cad_decide   the cylindrical decision procedure decq/decide for a closed
%                prenex formula with any quantifier prefix, and its
%                correctness decq_correct/decide_correct
%   cad_examples eleven sentences in two and three variables (lines, the circle
%                and the disc, a sum of squares, grouped binders, a constant
%                that settles the matrix for the witness search) decided by
%                (cad) through decide5
%   chain_sturm  the chain of branch_prs, read as the triple Sturm@sturm
%                expects, satisfies constructed_sturm_sequence?: the six
%                conditions, assembled from the structure of the chain and
%                from the step of sturm_step
%   sturm_step   one step of the symbolic sequence: minus b^k times the
%                pseudo-remainder, whose multiplier of the true remainder is
%                the square (b^k)^2, negative as the Sturm condition asks and
%                of known sign without branching on the sign of b
% Phase 6: the per-cell procedure (decision 13-15 of the plan)
%   sg_norm      evaluation by a sign function sg: [mpoly -> Sign3] instead
%                of a branch tree: normalization (snorm_sg), agreement with
%                select at msg(ys), read lists and determinism (X_det)
%   sg_chain     the sign remainder chain and Tarski queries by a sign
%                function (rchain_sg, tq_sg, mts_sg)
%   mpoly_div    exact division at the coefficient level: a fuel-bounded
%                quotient attempt (mdiv) and the check lexact (d * q = r by
%                meq after mnorm); nothing about mdiv needs proof
%   earr_ops     coefficient arrays through the list operations of
%                pseudo-division; proportional lists prop(l1, l2, c, ys) and
%                their passage through prem, sstep, normalization at ys and
%                the leading sign
%   sturm_step2  the reduced chain step sstep2: the pseudo-remainder divided
%                by cc^e when lexact confirms it, signed by the sign
%                function to be a negative multiple of the remainder,
%                otherwise sstep; at msg(ys) a positive multiple of sstep
%                (sstep2_prop), read-determined (sstep2_det)
%   sg_chain2    the chains with the reduced step, carrying the divisor of
%                Collins's reduced sequence (rchain2_sg); element by element
%                a positive multiple of rchain (rchain2_prop), so the Tarski
%                query and the count under sign conditions are those of
%                Phase 3 (tq2_sg_at, mts2_sg_at) and read-determined (_det)
%   sg_conj      the existence decision of a conjunction by a sign function
%                (ctree_sg, ctree_sg_sound), counting with mts2_sg
%   sg_conj2     the same, pruned: a nonzero constant refutes, identically
%                zero equations are dropped, a single equation is decided
%                on itself without the sum of squares (ctree2_sg_sound)
%   sg_svs       the realized sign vectors of a family by a sign function
%                (svs_sg_sound, svs_sg_complete, svs_det)
%   map_member   membership in a mapped list
%   mpoly_eqd    decidable equalities of polynomials and coefficient lists
%                (the ground evaluator cannot compare lists through =)
%   alg_fast     root isolation with the Sturm chain computed once (isoc_iso)
%   alg_sign2    the sign of a polynomial at an algebraic number without the
%                sum of squares: alg_sign decides q(value(a)) = 0 first by
%                counting the roots of p^2 + q^2, of twice the degree, and a
%                cell walk asks for thousands of signs at one point.
%                alg_sign2 counts the roots of q alone in the isolating
%                interval: with none, q keeps one sign there (sturm_sign) and
%                the sign is q at the midpoint, one rational evaluation.
%                alg_sign2_sign, alg_sign2_def
%   alg_count2   forward bounds on the root count: a root in a closed interval
%                forces a count of one (already in alg_count), and two or
%                three distinct roots force two or three, read off the
%                bijection of alg_count through the cardinality of the root
%                set (three_card, roots_finite, nroots_ge2, nroots_ge3). The
%                corrected sector test of sect_inv needs the three-root bound
%   cell1        the bottom level by root isolation: roots sorted without
%                repetition, rational samples between consecutive roots,
%                svs1_sound / svs1_complete; sortu evaluates alg_sortc_def's
%                sortsep (sortu_vl: the values of the insertion sort sortu0)
%   sect_inv     sectors of a sorted root list with their samples, numeric
%                sign invariance of a polynomial on a sector (inv_chk_sound);
%                sepf evaluates alg_sortc_def's sepcc (sepf_eq)
%   svs_pt       the realized sign vectors over one fibre at a RATIONAL sample
%                point, by root isolation instead of enumeration: svs_sg
%                branches three ways per family member, so it costs 3^|F|,
%                while specializing the family at the point (evalF) and
%                isolating the roots of the result is polynomial in the
%                family. evalF_svec relates the two, svs_pt_sound and
%                svs_pt_complete characterize the output as exactly
%                { svec(F, cons(x, rs)) : x real }
%   cad_lift     the cylindrical decision evaluated per cell: closure under
%                reads at the higher levels, sectors at level 1, lv_sound,
%                lv_complete, decq2_correct, decide2_correct (relative to
%                the completion flag)
%   subres       the subresultant chain and its principal coefficients: psc
%                is a det of a submatrix of the same Sylvester-style matrix,
%                so ring_det's det_eval gives the specialization theorem free
%                (psc_eval), exactly as for res. psc(f,g,0) = res(f,g)
%                (psc0_res), so cad_proj is the j = 0 slice of Collins.
%                A polynomial remainder sequence branches on the signs of its
%                leading coefficients -- that branching IS the 3^|F| of
%                rsc_sg -- while the psc are determinants and branch on
%                nothing, which is what a sign-determination argument needs
%   subres2      the FULL subresultant polynomial, not just its leading
%                coefficient (Stage C of item 1). Sturm counts sign changes of
%                CHAIN ENTRIES, and an entry is a polynomial whose sign at
%                -inf and +inf is its leading coefficient's sign times
%                (-1)^degree, so the whole Sres_j is needed. It is the same
%                determinant with the last column ranging over degrees 0..j,
%                so one matrix sresm2 covers both: sresm(f,g,j) is
%                sresm2(f,g,j,j) and psc is the j-th coefficient (psc_sres),
%                with evaluation commuting as before (sresc_eval)
%   nsc_scale    sign-change counting is blind to positive rescaling, which
%                is what licenses the approach to sh_count: NASALib's chain
%                entries come from adjusted_remainder, and
%                adjusted_remainder_def pins them only up to a POSITIVE
%                factor (= -cp * the true remainder, cp: posreal). So they
%                are the classical Sturm sequence up to positive factors --
%                harmless for the COUNT (nsc_scale, from
%                number_sign_changes_eq and sign_ext_mult), and it is why a
%                chain entry's leading-coefficient SIGN is well defined,
%                which is the quantity the psc correspondence is about
%   quad_sign    groundwork for psc_det? at degree <= 2, which is the case
%                every member of all four worked examples falls in.
%                Completing the square (qv_shift), the perfect-square
%                identity 4a*qv + discr = (2ay+b)^2 (qv_square) and the
%                extremum bounds it gives (qv_sq_ge, qv_min, qv_max), the
%                beyond-roots witness from quadratic_aux (bey, qv_bey,
%                bey_gt), and when ZERO is realized (qset_zero). NOTE
%                a /= 0 is required wherever discr appears: NASALib declares
%                discr on nonzero_real, and without the hypothesis the TCC is
%                the false FORALL a: a /= 0
%   quad_set     which signs a quadratic realizes: one small DIRECTIONAL
%                lemma per case (qset_pos_apos, qset_pos_aneg, qset_pos_lin,
%                qset_pos_cst, qset_pos_nec) rather than one ten-branch IFF,
%                which is what made the first attempt grind. The negative
%                case is free: qv(-a,-b,-c)(y) = -qv(a,b,c)(y) flips the
%                realized signs while discr is INVARIANT under negating all
%                three coefficients (qv_neg, discr_neg, qset_flip).
%                pos_prod is the helper that unstuck nearly everything here:
%                PVS's decision procedures are linear and will not chain
%                products of positives, so it goes through the prelude AXIOM
%                posreal_mult_closed
%   quad_det     psc_det? AT DEGREE <= 2: the realized sign SET is a function
%                of the sign data (qset_det). Every member of all four worked
%                examples has degree <= 2 in the main variable, so this is the
%                case that makes their fast decision proved. Uses a TOTAL
%                discriminant dsc, because NASALib's discr is declared on
%                nonzero_real while the hypothesis must cover a = 0 -- which
%                is precisely the case where a = b = 0 gives the same
%                discriminant sign as a genuine double root but a different
%                set. sign3_iff converts equal signs into equal comparisons
%                ONCE and is used four times; expanding sign3 inside the six
%                IFF characterisations instead makes ground blow up
%                combinatorially, and splitting the three sign cases
%                SEPARATELY keeps each assert small
%   fib_quad     psc_det? for a SINGLE-member family of degree <= 2, which
%                closes the chain end to end for the first time: with
%                delin_from_psc and dec2_sem, the two-quantifier projection
%                decision is PROVED CORRECT for single-polynomial families of
%                degree <= 2. meval3 specializes the member at the parameter
%                (stated over an EXPLICIT 3-element list -- with a
%                length(u) = 3 hypothesis meval unfolds into nested datatype
%                CASES and the proof does not converge), fib1_qset2 phrases
%                the fibre set as the 1-vectors of the realized signs so
%                qset_det applies with no skolem bookkeeping. The MULTI-member
%                case needs more: the fibre set is a set of VECTORS, so it
%                depends on how different members' roots interleave, which
%                per-member discriminants do not settle
%   projc_mem    what is actually IN Collins' projection: every coefficient
%                of a live member (cofs_mem, cofs_projc). fib1_det's
%                hypothesis is about a member's COEFFICIENT signs while
%                psc_det?'s is about projc's members, and these agree only
%                because projc really contains those polynomials -- a
%                structural fact about projc1 = cofs ++ chain that has to be
%                proved. cofs IS map(as_list) (cofs_map), which turns the
%                membership into a standard map lemma instead of an index
%                induction fighting nth
%   psc_quad1    the bookkeeping between fib_quad and delin_bridge:
%                svec_eq_sign (equal sign VECTORS give equal signs member by
%                member), pscs_zero (a psc chain always contains its j = 0
%                entry) and disc_projc (so the DISCRIMINANT is in projc, via
%                psc0_res identifying psc(f,f',0) with res(f,f') = disc f).
%                With projc_mem's coefficients, these are what let sect_svec
%                discharge fib1_det's hypothesis along a sector
%   disc_quad    what the projection's discriminant evaluates to at degree 2:
%                the 3x3 Sylvester determinant of (f, f') comes out to
%                -c2 * (c1^2 - 4*c0*c2), so sign(disc) = -sign(c2)*sign(dsc)
%                (disc_quad_eval). This is the last link for the single-member
%                result, and it is needed because sign(dsc) genuinely is NOT
%                determined by the coefficient signs alone -- (1,1,1) gives
%                dsc < 0 while (1,3,1) gives dsc > 0 -- so it has to come from
%                the projection, where disc lives
%   psc_det_quad THE OBLIGATION DISCHARGED, at degree <= 2 with one member.
%                psc_det?((: (: u0,u1,u2 :) :)) holds OUTRIGHT (psc_det_quad1):
%                equal sign vectors of projc give equal signs of every
%                coefficient and of disc (mem_sign over svec_eq_mem and
%                meval_as_list, with quad_mem0/1/2/d placing the four
%                polynomials inside projc); dsc_sign then divides disc's sign
%                by the leading coefficient's -- and when that vanishes reads
%                dsc = c1^2 off sign(c1) -- to recover sign(dsc); fib1_det
%                closes it.  quad_delin and quad_dec2 carry it through: the
%                two-quantifier projection decision is correct for such a
%                family with NO delineability assumption left, only side
%                conditions the executable code checks (roots sorted, gaps
%                separating, samples rational and inside their sector, root
%                isolation converged)
%   psc_det_ce   psc_det? is FALSE as a universal statement (2026-09-29,
%                GAP_PLAN.md Tier 0). For f = y^2-3y+2 and g = y-x, every
%                member of projc has the same sign at x = 1/2 and x = 3
%                (ce_svec, by evaluation), but the fibre over 1/2 realizes
%                (0,+) (y = 1) and the fibre over 3 does not, since f = 0 only
%                at y = 1, 2 where g < 0 (ce_fib_half, ce_fib_three); so
%                psc_det_false: NOT psc_det?(ceF). The order of the roots of
%                DIFFERENT members is not fixed by psc signs at two unrelated
%                points, only along a connected set. Nothing verified depends
%                on psc_det? (a hypothesis of delin_from_psc only; the
%                one-quadratic case psc_det_quad stands)
%   mpoly_swap  exchanging the two variables of a bivariate mpoly (item A).
%                mpoly's res eliminates the MAIN variable; lifting at an
%                algebraic sample has to eliminate the SECOND one, because
%                Res_x(p, f) for alpha a root of p is a polynomial in y with
%                RATIONAL coefficients whose roots include every y with
%                f(y, alpha) = 0 -- which is what keeps the candidate roots
%                over Q and makes an extension field unnecessary.  mswap is a
%                transpose of the rational coefficient matrix and mswap_eval
%                is its correctness; keeping the transpose at matrix level
%                makes that proof a sum interchange (mcons_bev) instead of a
%                double induction over the datatype
%   alg_lift    LIFTING AT AN ALGEBRAIC SAMPLE POINT, with no arithmetic in
%                Q(alpha).  svs_pt is typed on list[rat] and cannot represent
%                an algebraic coordinate at all, which is why every Bath
%                problem answers ok = FALSE.  svs_alg does the same job at an
%                algebraic alpha: cand(a, f) = Res_x(p, f) keeps the candidate
%                roots over Q (a SUPERSET, and supersets only refine the
%                decomposition); asgn is alg_sign2 on the rational univariate
%                f(y0, .); and a member's vanishing AT a candidate is read off
%                as a SIGN CHANGE between the two rational neighbours, which
%                under aok? -- lc and disc nonvanishing at alpha, so every
%                root simple -- is exact.  That replaces the
%                two-algebraic-coordinate zero test that forces Q(alpha).
%                SCOPE: one algebraic coordinate, so 2-variable-complete
%   alg_meas    what was measured before any of it was proved: the circle
%                lifts to +, 0, -, 0, +; the LINE shows the spurious candidate
%                carrying its neighbours' vector, which is the measurement
%                that justifies the whole design; the two together show
%                COINCIDING roots read off correctly per member; and aok?
%                refuses a degree drop instead of guessing
%   alg_sturm   STURM COUNTING AT AN ALGEBRAIC PARAMETER -- the piece that
%                was supposed to need a new theorem and did not.  alg_lift's
%                sign-change rule is exact only while every root is simple, so
%                it refuses a section at a DISCRIMINANT root; Sturm counting
%                does not care about multiplicity.  Everything it needs was
%                already proved: chain_sturm's fchain_sturm (the symbolic
%                chain read at ANY real valuation IS a
%                constructed_sturm_sequence?, and its condition fok asks only
%                for the LEADING COEFFICIENT), sg_chain's rchain_sg_at (the
%                same chain from a SIGN FUNCTION, which makes it executable
%                through alg_sign2), nsc_scale (counting signs is counting
%                values) and earr_eval_dg (a chain entry at rational y0 and
%                x = alpha is a rational univariate at alpha).  acount counts
%                the roots in (y0, y1]; acount_zero and acount_pos are what
%                bisection needs, and alc? -- length, leading coefficient,
%                fuel -- is the whole condition
%   alg_sturm2  the counts at +-infinity, so isolation has a starting
%                interval and a termination test.  A Cauchy bound is not
%                available -- it needs MAGNITUDES of the coefficients at
%                alpha, where alg_sign2 gives only signs -- but Sturm's
%                unbounded theorems need none either: at +-infinity the
%                sequence is read off the chain's LEADING COEFFICIENTS alone,
%                which arr_lc turns into meval(llast(.)).  atotal splits as
%                aleft + acount + aright, each part is >= 0, so acount =
%                atotal forces the other two to zero and acaptures says the
%                interval has every root.  That is the widening test, with no
%                bound on any coefficient anywhere
%   alg_lift2   svs_alg with STURM COUNTING in place of the sign-change rule.
%                A member vanishes somewhere in (r1, r2] exactly when
%                acount(k, a, f, r1, r2) > 0, and Sturm does not care about
%                multiplicity -- so the discriminant condition disappears and
%                what is left, aok1b, is: constant in the main variable, or
%                else length >= 2, LEADING coefficient nonzero at alpha, fuel,
%                and a nonzero candidate polynomial
%   alg_lift2_ex the measurement: ok2a refuses x^2 + y^2 - 2 (its sections are
%                the circle's vertical tangents, where the fibre has a
%                repeated root) and ok2b accepts it and decides both
%                directions correctly, while still agreeing with the
%                sign-change rule where that rule worked
%   alg_bis     THE LIFTING ON BISECTION ALONE: no resultant anywhere.
%                alg_lift and alg_lift2 place the section points at the roots
%                of Res_x(p, f), which leaves one step an argument rather than
%                a theorem -- that those candidates CONTAIN every root.  Here
%                acount finds the roots itself: bwide doubles until
%                acount = atotal (acaptures then says every root is inside --
%                no Cauchy bound, no magnitudes), bint splits until every gap
%                holds at most one root of the product, and bwalk reads a
%                vector at each separator plus one per gap holding a root,
%                where a member is zero exactly when acount says it has a
%                root there.  Two things measurement forced: the product must
%                be over the LIVE members only (on x^2 + y^2 - 2 the second
%                member IS x^2 - 2, so including it makes the product vanish
%                identically at exactly the sections), and it must be
%                STRIPPED of structural trailing zeros (lmul pads, so llast
%                came back mconst(0) and alc? refused)
%   alg_bis_ex  the same answers as the resultant route, measured, including
%                the DOUBLE root: at x = sqrt(2) the fibre is y^2 and
%                svs_bis reports (: (1,0), (0,0), (1,0) :)
%   sturm_sg    Sturm counting THROUGH A SIGN ORACLE (FINISH_PLAN S1).  The
%                counts of alg_sturm/alg_sturm2 restated for an arbitrary
%                sg: SG and proved at sg = msg(ys) for ys of ANY length:
%                cnt_nneg/zero/pos, cnt_one (a count of one means the root is
%                unique), left/right_zero, captures.  A chain entry is read at
%                a rational y0 as sg(sub1(c, y0)), sub1 substituting the main
%                variable inside the mpoly ring, so no variable swap is needed
%   walk_def    the lifting as a WALK over rational separators through a sign
%                oracle (FINISH_PLAN S2): walk reads a vector at each
%                separator and one per gap holding a root of the live product;
%                wok? is everything it needs from the separators, decided
%                through the oracle; seps is an UNTRUSTED search for them
%   walk_ok     walk_fib: wok?(F, msg(ys), Y) implies the walk lists exactly
%                fib(F, ys) -- for ANY separators that pass, at ANY real
%                valuation.  Members that strip below length 2 are constant
%                along the fibre, live ones vanish only where the product
%                does, so the vector is constant off the product's roots
%                (svec_same_live, without sector_rep's assumption that no
%                member vanishes identically); a gap with count one has a
%                unique root (cnt_one), at which a member is zero exactly when
%                its own count is positive (sgn_at)
%   loc_const   a predicate that is LOCALLY constant on a closed interval is
%                constant on it (FINISH_PLAN S4, T4): the connectedness of the
%                line by a least-upper-bound argument, nothing else imported
%   sign_pers   SIGN PERSISTENCE (T1): finitely many polynomials, each nonzero
%                at x0 or zero for every value of the main variable, keep
%                their signs near x0 with the other variables xs held fixed --
%                msg(cons(z, xs)) agrees with msg(cons(x0, xs)) on the list
%                (msg_pers), from the continuity of pf
%   walk_rd     what the walk READS, split in two (T2): INTRINSIC reads wi_rd
%                (chain construction reads and leading coefficients, no
%                separator in them) and ENTRY reads we_rd (chain entries and
%                members at the separators).  walk_det: oracles that agree on
%                both walk alike and pass or fail wok? alike
%   sturm_sg2   the converses of sturm_sg's counting theorems: cnt_root (a
%                root makes the count positive), cnt_two (a count of two gives
%                two distinct roots), left_pos / right_pos, and how the counts
%                add (cnt_add, left_step, right_step)
%   sep_exist   SEPARATORS EXIST AT EVERY POINT (T3): for f with nonzero
%                leading coefficient at ys and a finite list of polynomials to
%                avoid there are increasing rationals at which none of them
%                vanishes, each gap holds at most one root of f, and no root
%                lies outside.  No root bound, no enumeration: induction on
%                the unbounded counts for the ends, on the bounded count for
%                the middle, density inside a root-free interval (avoid)
%   walk_transfer  THE TRANSFER THEOREM (T5).  fib_local: where the intrinsic
%                reads keep their signs, the fibre set is locally constant --
%                separators exist at x0, the entry reads are nonzero there and
%                persist, so nearby oracles agree on all the walk reads and
%                walk_fib gives the same set.  fib_const: on a CONVEX such set
%                it is constant.  Stated along ONE coordinate of a point
%                cons(x, xs) (S5a); xs = null is the one-parameter case.  This is fibre-set
%                invariance certified at run time (weaker than classical
%                delineability; GAP_PLAN.md 1.3), with no subresultants; the entry reads are never certified,
%                because no constant separator stays clear of an unbounded or
%                colliding root curve (measured: 164 roots and ok = FALSE)
%   cad_fast_def  the FAST two-quantifier decision decw: sectors of the
%                projection closed under the intrinsic reads only (iclos),
%                lifting by the walk (svs_w), certificate icert?
%   cad_fast    decw_correct: decw`ok implies decw`val IFF the sentence --
%                unconditional in the sense of decide2_correct.  Per sector:
%                the walk's list is the fibre set at the sample (sect_walk),
%                the sector is delineable (sect_delin, from fib_const and
%                inv_chk_sound), so the inner fold is the inner quantifier at
%                every point (sect_sem); the sectors cover the line
%   cad_fast_ex  measured: ex_line ONE root sector and ~1 s where decide2
%                takes 48 s over 43 sectors; circle, disc, the meeting curves
%                and the discriminant-root sections (both refused or slow
%                before) about a second each, negative controls right
%   cad_decide3_def, cad_decide3  decide3 = decw when its certificate passes
%                at two quantifiers, decide2 otherwise; decide3_correct has
%                decide2_correct's statement, and (cad) cited it until
%                cad_decide4 (2026-09-21)
%   tower_def   the ORACLE TOWER, executable (FINISH_PLAN S5): a level below
%                the outermost is a walk through the oracle of the level
%                above; at a separator the cell's oracle is sg o sub1(., y_j),
%                at a section a bounded Tarski query tq_b against the member
%                that vanishes there.  dec3m: a MEASURING three-quantifier
%                decision (ex_three 4 s; decide2 does not finish)
%   tower_ok    tower: the bounded Tarski query through msg(ys) is the sign of
%                g at the one root of l0 in the gap (sturm_tarski_ends on
%                tchain_sturm: only l0 need be nonzero at the ends)
%   cell_ok     the cells of an inner level are read correctly: at a separator
%                sg_pt(msg(ys), y0) IS msg(cons(y0, ys)) (pt_fib); at a section
%                every sound query returns the sign at (beta, ys) (qsec_val,
%                from tower), so when all the inner walk's queries are sound
%                walk_det makes it the walk at (beta, ys) (sec_fib); and at a
%                gap the walk certified every query is sound
%                (qsec_ok_complete, allok_complete: COMPLETENESS_PLAN C4)
%   level_ok    TWO INNER QUANTIFIERS AT A FIXED POINT (S5d, claim A).
%                inner2_sem: in2ok?(F, msg(ys)) implies inner2 IFF
%                sem((: q2, q3 :), F, Psi, ys), at any real ys.  The cells
%                cover the line: on an interval where the level's product has
%                no root the inner truth is that at the separator inside it
%                (T_same = fib_const in the y-direction; its hypothesis holds
%                because the inner walk's intrinsic reads are MEMBERS of the
%                level's family, mem_same); the rest mirrors walk_ok one level
%                up with the truth value in place of the sign vector
%   cad3_def    the THREE-QUANTIFIER decision decw3, executable: per outer
%                sector the level below is inner2; the certificate is in2ok?
%                at the sample plus the S4 certificate for the level's family
%                on the sector.  Measured: ok = TRUE and the right value on
%                seven sentences, ex_three included, 0-9 s each
%   joint_cont  a polynomial keeps its sign near a point of the plane of its
%                first two variables (unif: Horner induction; joint_sign;
%                joint_pers for a list) -- for the entry reads at a section,
%                which moves in both coordinates
%   cover       the cells of a level cover the line, on its own: every y sees
%                a separator across a root-free interval (atsep?) or is the
%                section of a gap (atsec?)
%   closed_pt   "the inner walk's intrinsic reads are members of P" at EVERY
%                point: rdin? depends on a point only through P's sign vector
%                there (rdin_vec), S4's fib_const for P gives equal SETS of
%                sign vectors over the sector, so cl_transfer needs no
%                correspondence between cells; cl_cells gets it at the sample
%                from the executable certificate
%   walk_same   walk_transfer's fibre lemma between ANY two valuations, not
%                just two that differ in the first coordinate: if the walk
%                passes at vs0 and the oracles agree on the intrinsic reads and
%                on wnz (the entry reads that do not vanish), the fibres over
%                vs1 and vs0 are the same set of sign vectors
%   root_cont   a simple root moves continuously, by counting: lev? (a level
%                family over a convex S) keeps cnt on a fixed gap constant near
%                x0 (cnt_local); ends_exist shrinks the gap around the root;
%                root_near puts the root over x within eta of the root over x0
%   pt_local    local constancy at ONE cell: near (beta0, x0), points with the
%                same sign vector on P have the same inner fibre set
%                (joint_pers + walk_same), so the inner truth T is the same
%   cells_local all cells at once, by induction over the separators: for x
%                near x0 in the sector the walk over P agrees (wagree?) and
%                every cell -- separator or section -- has the same inner truth
%                over x as over x0 (cells_eq?); sections by root_near
%   inner_const the two inner quantifiers do not vary along an outer sector:
%                V_sub (cover + T_same_r + gap_sec) turns cells_eq? into "every
%                inner truth value over one point occurs over the other",
%                G_local is that near x0, G_const is loc_const on the sector
%   cad3        THE THREE-QUANTIFIER DECISION IS CORRECT: decw3_correct.  Per
%                sector, inner2_sem reads the two inner quantifiers at the
%                sample and G_const carries that truth along the sector
%                (inv? from inv_sect over the level's family, cert? from
%                cert_w, CL? from cl_cells); fold3_sem is foldw_sem's fold
%   cad_decide4_def, cad_decide4  the decision (cad) ran until N10: decw
%                for two quantifiers, decw3 for three, decide2 as the fallback
%                and for every other prefix; decide4_correct (decide5 has no
%                fallback: decn_u for three or more quantifiers, decw then
%                decn_u for two, decq2 for one)
%   cad_examples3  three quantifiers through (cad), seconds each: six TRUE
%                sentences (sphere over the disc, the cone, a sum of
%                squares, grouped binders) and two FALSE ones refuted by
%                (cad -1)
%   walk_od     the walk through an ORACLE DESCRIPTOR (a rational point, a
%                point with algebraic coordinates, a section over a descriptor,
%                or a separator over a tower point) and a
%                CHAIN TABLE: chains built once per level and reused at any
%                cell whose oracle agrees with the recorded signs of their
%                reads (i_det), entry signs at a rational point by meval
%                (sent_sign); every function proved equal to its walk_def
%                original at the oracle the descriptor denotes (walk_t_eq,
%                wok_wt_eq, svs_t_eq).  Ten cells: 15 s -> 5 s, of which
%                the table is 5 s
%   rd_norm     reads normalized to a canonical scalar multiple (divide by
%                the leading rational): equal-up-to-a-constant reads of
%                different pair chains coincide in the closure; the value
%                scales by a fixed nonzero constant (rd_norm_eval), which is
%                all sign invariance needs
%   qsec_rd     the reads of one section query (secp, the normalized c,
%                the Tarski chain, its entries at the two ends of the gap):
%                qsec_det -- the query's ok flag and value depend on the
%                oracle only through them; qs_det for a read list
%   qsec_lift   a section oracle over an APPROXIMATE base oracle: if the base
%                agrees with the point on the queries' reads, every certified
%                query answers the sign at the section root (allok_agree2)
%   ztrunc      degree drops (2026-09-27): tz(sg, l) is a coefficient list
%                without the top coefficients the oracle says vanish; at a
%                point with a right oracle it has the member's value above the
%                point (tz_eval), a nonzero top (tz_top), and it is decided by
%                the coefficients' signs (tz_agree); crd(F), every coefficient
%   towern_def, towern_od  (imported through odreads and decc_def -- towern_def
%                also through tclf and col_tower_def -- not directly)
%                the n-LEVEL EXECUTABLE: one tower of families per
%                outer sector, cells through oracle descriptors and chain
%                tables, the members identically zero at a cell filtered out
%                and every other member truncated at the cell (feff, ztrunc),
%                their sign 0 put back (ins0); decn_o, with the
%                recursive certificate okqs of every oracle answer (3i).
%                Four quantifiers in 1-16 s.  Correctness: decn_ok.
%   odreads     the executable's read lists ARE the trusted walk's at the
%                walked (effective) family, plus every member coefficient
%                (wi_rd_t_eq, we_rd_p_eq), so agreement on them transports to
%                walk_det / wok_exist (wi_agree, we_agree)
%   mpar        continuity of meval in ALL coordinates (near?: same length,
%                every coordinate within d) through prod_cont, and so
%                persistence of finitely many signs in many parameters
%                (msg_mpers): the n-level proof moves every coordinate at once
%   zfam        the EFFECTIVE family zf(F, ws) (members not identically zero
%                at ws, each truncated at ws by ztrunc's tz; the executable's
%                feff), insz putting their zeros
%                back, the reads wrs (all member coefficients ++ the walk's
%                intrinsic reads), certz?, and rdz?(F, G, p): the reads of the
%                F-walk at p are, after rd_norm, MEMBERS of the family above
%                (the executable's clok?), so their signs anywhere are read
%                off G's sign vector (rdz_agree, rdz_vec); invz? and
%                fib_constz (S4's fib_const with identically-zero members);
%                rf_invz (the hypothesis one level down on a root-free
%                interval)
%   mwalk       the walk near a point with every coordinate moving: the
%                separators still pass, gaps, separator and section vectors
%                unchanged (near_cells); the fibre is locally constant
%                (fib_mlocal: the base of the tower induction)
%   mroot       root_near in many parameters (mroot_near): the member owning
%                the section root keeps exactly one root between rational
%                ends near it (cnt_mlocal); root_cont's lemmas reused at a
%                one-point set
%   cvl         the CELL VALUE LIST of a level: a truth function read at the
%                separators and at the roots of the gaps (secbeta), in the
%                walk's order; if the function does not change across
%                root-free intervals its members are exactly its values
%                (cvl_all, by cover), so the fold is the quantifier
%                (bfold_cvl); equal cells give equal lists (cvl_eq)
%   tclf        the CLOSURE of a tower (CLF: at every point over ws each
%                level's effective family is certified and its walk's reads
%                are members of the family above), prefixes, the all-EX
%                sentence (sem_ex), and the three height-n statements: CBh
%                constancy on a sector, LCh local constancy with every
%                coordinate moving, CTh closure transfer along a sector;
%                ct_step (CTh from CBh below) and cb_step (CBh from LCh, CTh
%                by loc_const)
%   tlc         LCh by induction on the height (lc_base from fib_mlocal,
%                lc_step: the walk agrees near the point, the cell value lists
%                are equal, root-free constancy of the inner truth, rfc_tw)
%   tower_sem   tower_all: CBh, LCh, CTh at every height by strong induction;
%                tower_cb (the inner quantifiers have one truth value on an
%                outer sector) and tower_ct (the closure certified at the
%                sample holds on the whole sector)
%   oddef       what a descriptor denotes (den: sections by secbeta, the root
%                of the gap) and when it is well formed (wf?); okqs_agree:
%                the executable's recursive certificate okqs makes the oracle
%                right on the reads it certifies; the cells' descriptors
%                denote the cell points (opt_den, osec_den); feff is zf
%   innern_sem  CLAIM A on descriptors: a checked tower (okn_o) at a well
%                formed descriptor reads the inner quantifiers' truth at the
%                point it denotes, and certifies the closure there (induction
%                on the tower: lvl_exact, cellsf_cvl, clf_level, ia_step)
%   decn_ok     THE n-LEVEL DECISION IS CORRECT: decn_correct.  Per outer
%                sector the tower has k levels ending with F (lvln_shape),
%                claim A at the sample (sectn_sem), the top family's reads
%                invariant on an open sector from inv_chk (inv_topreads), so
%                tower_cb carries the truth along it; foldn_sem is the fold
%   cad_decide5_def, cad_decide5  the decision (cad) runs in a theory that
%                does not import cad_decide8 (decide8 otherwise): decn_u (decn_o
%                from u = 0, raising u until the certificate holds) for
%                three or more quantifiers, decw then decn_u for two,
%                decide2 for one; no exponential fallback; decide5_correct.
%                complete_all: it always answers (decide5_decides)
%   cad_decide6_def, cad_decide6  a decision with no failure flag
%                (COMPLETENESS_PLAN C1): decide5 when its certificate passes,
%                else the complete but exponential decide of cad_decide;
%                decide6_correct, for every closed prenex formula, no
%                hypothesis.  (cad :complete? t) uses the fallback
%   complete1   the one-quantifier decision is complete (COMPLETENESS_PLAN
%                C3): decq2's certificate at one quantifier is gaps_ok on the
%                sorted roots, which cell1's total refinement gapw now makes
%                TRUE on every strictly increasing list (gaps_ok_incr);
%                decide5_complete1: decide5 answers every closed formula with
%                at most one quantifier
%   sturm_tarski_ends  the Tarski query with only the first polynomial
%                nonzero at the ends (COMPLETENESS_PLAN C4): NASALib's
%                sturm_tarski asks it of every entry of the constructed Sturm
%                sequence; sturm_tarski_ends moves each end inward past no root
%                (ends_inward), applies sturm_tarski, and carries the counts back
%                (tsig_near, from nsc_edge_diff: tss_opposite, tss_no_two_zeros)
%                and the root sets (NSol_union_top, nsol_none)
%   od_exact    every oracle of a well-formed descriptor is exact
%                (COMPLETENESS_PLAN C4): with the section queries complete
%                (cell_ok's allok_complete), the executable's recursive
%                certificate okqs holds at every well-formed descriptor on any
%                read list (okqs_complete), so with oddef's okqs_agree
%                osg(od) = msg(den(od)) (wf_exact): every walk below a
%                certified level runs at the point's own oracle
%   cert_all    the walk's leading-coefficient certificate holds at every
%                point (COMPLETENESS_PLAN C5): certz_all, from tz_top (the
%                effective members' tops are nonzero), zc_iff/strip_at (a
%                coefficient zero everywhere is structurally zero and strip
%                removes it) and prodl_above/prodl_top (the product has no
%                coefficient above the degree sum, and there it is the product
%                of the leading coefficients)
%   walk_fuel   seps_n: the separator search with an explicit fuel n
%                (seps_n_fuel: seps is seps_n at sfuel2)
%   walk_count  counting for the search's completeness (COMPLETENESS_PLAN
%                C6): cnt_ge (a Sturm count is at least the number of distinct
%                roots listed), cnt_le_total, nzat_P_iff, and stop_ok -- the
%                bisection's stop test passes on any gap where the product of
%                the live members has at most one distinct root, with no
%                condition on the chains
%   walk_search the walk's search succeeds at every exact oracle, and more
%                fuel changes nothing (C6): spick/sint/swide each return
%                their first success (spick_mono, sint_mono, swide_mono);
%                spick_exists (distinct probes cannot all be roots),
%                sint_exists (nested induction on the product's count and a
%                shrink budget, sint_inner -- no global root separation),
%                swide_exists (P_bounded, leftok_P/rightok_P); seps_ok: some
%                fuel N makes wok? hold and every n >= N gives the same
%                separators
%   list_flat   generic: flat (a member of a member is a member of the
%                flattening), pre?/prefs (every prefix is listed) (imported
%                through read_univ, read_fam and aug_univ, not directly)
%   read_univ   the reads of a Sturm chain lie in one finite list at EVERY
%                oracle (C7): sstep2 picks one of three candidates
%                (s3cands), snorm_sg leaves a prefix (snorm_pre), so by
%                induction on the chain bound rd_in, el_in, i_rd_in
%   read_fam    the intrinsic reads of a level's walk, at any cell and with
%                any valid table, lie in WU(F) fixed by the family: the
%                effective family is one of effs(F) (feff_in), and wu(P)
%                bounds a fixed one's reads (wi_in); rw_in for the closure
%   tower_univ  the tower closure stays in finite universes (C7): tul gives
%                each level's universe from the innermost up (the initial
%                family and rwall of the level below's), tinv? (initial
%                family + new members, no repeats) is kept by every round
%                (treads_sub, zipadd_inv), so tsize <= tbnd(T0); tclos_fix,
%                lvln_fix: u >= tbnd(T0) gives a tower one more round leaves
%                unchanged (fixp?)
%   clos_ok     at a fixpoint the closure check passes at every cell of
%                every level: cells_o lists a walk's cells (cellsf_every,
%                cellsr_has), zipadd_zsub, fixp_clk (clk?: cellok_o at every
%                cell)
%   nclos_ok    the outer closure ends with every sector's reads passing
%                inv_chk: a member's reads pass (memb_inv, from
%                allroots_complete, sect_noroot, inv_chk_complete), so bad
%                reads are new (nbads_new) and lie in rwall(tul(T0))
%                (nbads_in); nclos_fix: with enough fuel no bad reads remain
%   ev_pred, ev_nat  "eventually" over the escalation parameter u (C8):
%                ev?, evc? (eventually constant), ev_list over a finite
%                list, lim (the value an eventually constant map settles on)
%                (ev_nat is imported through clos_ev, tower_ev,
%                decn_complete and others, not directly)
%   lev_ev      one level at a well-formed cell: from some u on lvok_o holds
%                and the separators are fixed (lev_ok: wf_exact, certz_all,
%                seps_ok, okqs_complete); the cells do not depend on the
%                valid table (cells_valid) and are well formed (cells_wf);
%                cellsr_cong
%   tower_ev    okn_o = lvall? (walks) AND clk? (closure) (okn_split); by
%                induction on the tower, for eventually constant valid
%                tables, lvall? eventually holds and treads_o is eventually
%                constant (tower_ev); the tables are (tables_evc)
%   clos_ev     the closures settle: tclos_evc / tclos_stop / lvln_evc,
%                okn_ev (each sector's closed tower passes okn_o for large
%                u), nbads_evc / nclos_stop / nclos_evc_fuel
%   decn_complete  THE n-LEVEL DECISION IS COMPLETE: decn_ev -- for every
%                family and at least two quantifiers decn_o(u, ..)`ok for
%                every large u; decn_complete
%   decide_u_def, decide_u  decn_u: decn_o from a given u, raising u until
%                the certificate holds; terminates by decn_ev (the bound is
%                only in the measure); decn_u_correct, decn_u_complete
%   complete_all  decide5 (what (cad) runs without cad_decide8) is complete: decq5_complete,
%                decide5_complete, and decide5_decides -- decide5 answers
%                every closed formula with its truth, no hypothesis
%   cad_examples4  FOUR quantifiers through (cad), seconds each: three TRUE
%                sentences with alternating prefixes and a FALSE one refuted
%                by (cad -1)
%   chain_rd    a Sturm chain and the reads of its construction in ONE pass
%                (rchain2_cr, schain_rd_eq); walk_od's chain-table entries
%                (mkce) go through it (PERF_PLAN P1: they built each chain
%                step about seven times)
%   pl_shift_def  the Taylor shift of a rational polynomial (executable,
%                2026-10-01): pshift(pl, m) at t is pl at m + t
%   pl_shift     pshift_sem, by NASALib's psum / pscal
%   alg_isign   the sign at an algebraic number by an interval-Horner
%                enclosure over its (refined) isolating interval, then over the
%                query shifted to the middle of the interval (pshift), with
%                alg_sign2 as the fallback (alg_isign_sign, from NASALib's
%                Add_inclusion / Mult_inclusion); the mix oracle of walk_od
%                uses it (PERF_PLAN P4)
%   sturm_fast  NASALib's Sturm root count with every chain element evaluated
%                once, by Horner (hornz_poly, hornA_poly); rcf_eq: it is
%                roots_closed_int; alg_count's nroots_a, sect_inv's nroots_c
%                and alg_isolate's nrh (the half-open count of the root
%                isolation) use it (PERF_PLAN R3)
%   poly_gcd    Euclid on rational polynomials: a point is a common root of
%                p and q exactly when it is a root of their gcd (pgcd_root,
%                through pmod_root; fuel-bounded, no termination argument);
%                alg_zero's zero_at decides "q vanishes at an algebraic
%                number" through it, the sum of squares as the fallback
%                (PERF_PLAN P6b: 0.31 s -> ~1 ms per test)
%   cad_star_ex  (cad *): the polynomial content of a whole sequent -- the
%                usable antecedents and consequents (real quantifier prefix,
%                polynomial comparisons; other real subterms as unknown
%                reals, other formulas skipped) decided as one closed
%                formula and applied by case; 15 sequents (CADSTAR_PLAN.md)
%   cad_endgame  the endgame of (cad) (ENDGAME_PLAN.md): sign3(x) = 1 / 0 / -1
%                IFF x > 0 / x = 0 / x < 0 (cadz_s3_pos/zero/neg).  The
%                strategy restates each atom of the goal as the sign
%                conditions the decision reads, so the goal and the formula
%                from decide5_correct share their atoms and bddsimp closes
%                the goal -- no case split on the signs (the old lift-if
%                endgame, kept as the fallback, is exponential in the atoms)
%   cad_endgame_ex  tests of that endgame: every comparison and connective,
%                TRUE and FALSE, a repeated atom, division by a numeral,
%                alternation, an irrational witness, a refuted hypothesis
%   cad_bath    problems of the Bath CAD example bank: bath_08 (Random B
%                Grobner from Buchberger-Hong, three quantifiers, a degree-7
%                member) refuted by (cad -1), about 10 s for the whole proof;
%                bath_02 proved and bath_05 refuted by fixing one variable
%                (the witness search's reduction).  Of the other closed
%                problems (measurements, not lemmas), (cad) settles 01, 03 and
%                04 by a model search and 07 by fixing one variable, in under
%                13 s each; 09, 10 and 12 are not
%                settled, and the decision alone does not finish in 90 s on
%                02-05, 07, 09, 10 and 12
%   cad_examples2  larger two-variable problems through (cad), seconds each:
%                a cubic, AM-GM, the circle meeting a parabola, a quartic, and
%                a FALSE sentence refuted in the antecedent by (cad -1)
%   alg_dec     the two-quantifier decision with ALGEBRAIC samples: no rsv,
%                no ratsamp?, no fallback, since sample(s) is an Alg for every
%                sector and rat_alg covers the rational ones
%   alg_dec_ex  what that buys and where it stops, both measured.  f_meet
%                (y - x with y^2 - 2) has IRRATIONAL sections at +-sqrt(2)
%                that the rational route refuses and this one certifies.
%                f_rad2 (the circle of radius sqrt(2)) has its sections AT
%                DISCRIMINANT ROOTS, where the fibre has a repeated root, so
%                no member changes sign there and aok? correctly refuses --
%                the three open sectors pass, the two sections do not.  That
%                residual case needs the sign of a polynomial at a point with
%                two algebraic coordinates; alg_lift2, alg_bis and the n-level
%                walk decide it (sh_count, sketched for it in sturm_habicht,
%                was left with the psc route)
%   cad_decn    dec at ANY number of levels computes the semantics
%                (decn_sem), and pdecide's val field with it (pdecide_sem).
%                dec2_sem settled two quantifiers; this is the general form,
%                and it is what a strategy must cite to emit a proof rather
%                than a measurement.  Everything the theorem needs from the
%                geometry is collected in ONE predicate, decok?, stated along
%                every path of sectors the fold actually walks -- roots sorted,
%                samples inside their sectors, the value of everything inside
%                constant along each sector, and the same one level down.
%                fold_quant is the combinatorial core (a fold over sectors IS
%                a quantifier over the reals); qfoldS_cong is what lets the
%                induction hypothesis, available only on the sectors decok?
%                ranges over, be used under the fold
%   sturm_habicht non-defectiveness, and the shape of Stage C's remaining
%                theorem. NON-DEFECTIVENESS IS ITSELF A SIGN CONDITION ON THE
%                psc (ndef_signs), so two points where projc(F) has the same
%                signs are either both defective or both not, and the runtime
%                check ITEM1_PLAN.md calls for is just "every psc nonzero" --
%                which sect_svec already makes constant along a sector, so
%                nothing extra is computed. sh_count, the correspondence
%                between NASALib's integer pseudo-remainder sequence and the
%                psc, was only sketched (in sturm_habicht's comments) and left
%                with the psc route (psc_det_ce)
%   cad_projc    Collins' projection: coefficients, plus the psc chain of each
%                member against its derivative and of each pair. [Note
%                2026-10-01, after cad_projc's own 2026-09-29 note: without
%                Collins' reducta, so Collins-like, not Collins' operator;
%                Collins' N-reducta projection is cad_projn_def's projn,
%                proved delineating by col_stack's collins_stack with
%                cad_projn's projn_ok.] Measured
%                barely larger than McCallum's and, decisively, adding NO
%                roots below (cad_meas3: collins_cost), so the sector count
%                and the whole speed advantage survive a Collins-like
%                operator (projc); the operator proved delineating is projn
%   cad_pdec     the projection decision at any number of levels: tower(m,F)
%                is (: proj^m(F), ..., F :), and dec folds the outermost
%                quantifier over the sectors of the outermost family
%                specialized at the coordinates chosen so far, prepending
%                each sector's sample, handing the innermost fibre to svs_pt.
%                Sample points must be exact: alg? demands lb < ub STRICTLY so
%                an exact rational is never a collapsed interval; rsamp takes
%                -c0/c1 when the defining polynomial is linear and otherwise
%                bisects for a midpoint that evaluates to zero, which PROVES
%                it is the root (rroot_exact, rroot_in, rsamp_value,
%                rsv_exact). okpts checks that over every sector visited.
%                CORRECTNESS IS NOT CLAIMED: that the projection's sectors
%                are cells over which the fibre is invariant is delineability
%   cad_pdec_ex  that decision on the worked examples: pdec_examples (all four
%                correct with ok = TRUE, 0.20-0.34 s) and pdec_controls
%                (FALSE on false formulas over the same families)
%   cad_meas     the projection against the read closure on the worked
%                examples, by ground evaluation: proj_not_inv1 (the projection
%                fails inv1?), seeded_inv1, routes_agree, three_works,
%                controls; also sgn, used by alg_dec_ex (imported through
%                cad_pdec_ex and alg_dec_ex, not directly)
%   sect_svec    a sector carries one sign vector of the family that defined
%                it: on a sector of sects(rts(P)) every member of P keeps its
%                sign, so svec(P,(: x :)) = svec(P,(: r :)) for x and the
%                sector's rational sample. This is the first half of
%                delin_projc and is about the DECOMPOSITION, not about
%                delineability. cell1's svec_between gives it from "no
%                isolated root lies between", and below_first / above_last
%                cover the outer sectors; the mid and above cases needed
%                their own inductions because gsects has no inverse-structure
%                lemma (gsects_mid_mem, gsects_mid_noroot, gsects_above_mem,
%                gsects_above_max, gsects_above_noroot, gsects_kind), plus
%                in_between for sector convexity
%   delin_bridge from the psc statement to delineability: psc_det? says two
%                points where projc(F) has the same signs have the same fibre
%                set, and delin_from_psc turns that into delin? on every
%                sector -- which is what dec2_sem consumes. Chaining them,
%                psc_det? ALONE implies the two-quantifier projection decision
%                is correct. [Corrected 2026-09-29: psc_det? is FALSE for some
%                two-member families (psc_det_ce), so it is not an open
%                statement to prove; the open obligation of this route is the
%                sector-local delin_projc, which needs connectedness.] The route goes through the sector's
%                RATIONAL sample because cell1's svec_between, and so
%                sect_svec, compares a real against a rational; at an at
%                sector both points collapse to the root
%   spec_deg     Stage B of item 1: degree is determined by sign data alone.
%                deg(pl) is the largest index carrying a nonzero entry, so it
%                depends only on WHICH coefficients vanish, and sign3 decides
%                exactly that (sign3_zero_spec_deg). Proved as one inequality plus
%                antisymmetry (deg_le, deg_signs): the hypothesis is symmetric
%                in p and q, and proving the equality head-on splits into a
%                dozen sign branches while each half of the inequality is a
%                few steps
%   cad_delin    delineability stated as a PREDICATE (delin?: the fibre set
%                does not vary along a sector) and the decision proved
%                correct GIVEN it: dec2_sem is the projection analogue of
%                qfoldS_sem, which needs inv1? -- the read-closure condition
%                that forces 21 root sectors where the projection leaves 1.
%                Nothing is assumed: delin? is a definition, dec2_sem a
%                proof. The reduction works because svs_pt is ALREADY proved
%                to compute the fibre set exactly (svs_pt_fib), so one
%                quantifier's semantics sees only that set (qfold_fib,
%                sem1_fib, via fib_swap) and delineability moves it from the
%                sample to the whole sector (sect_sem_delin).
%                cad_pdec also carries pdecq, the HYBRID: decq2 with proj in
%                place of clos1 and svsec's dispatch at level 2, so algebraic
%                coordinates go through the proved svs_sg/alg_sign2 route and
%                no rational sample is needed (hence no ok flag). Correct and
%                fast on the four examples, but it TIMES OUT on the Bath
%                problems, which have an algebraic coordinate at the
%                outermost level -- svs_pt is typed on list[rat] and cannot
%                represent one, so they enter the 3^|F| branch at once.
%                Exact arithmetic in an algebraic extension is required
%                STILL OPEN: delin_projc, that every sector of projc(F)
%                satisfies delin?. [Corrected 2026-09-29: this was described
%                as equivalent to "two univariate families with the same psc
%                signs realize the same sign vectors"; that pointwise form is
%                false (psc_det_ce), and delin_projc is strictly weaker. Note
%                also that delin? is FIBRE-SET invariance, weaker than
%                classical delineability (GAP_PLAN.md 1.3).] It concerns only
%                this conditional projection route; decide5 does not use it
%   cad_proj     the McCallum-shaped projection operator: all coefficients,
%                the discriminant of each member of degree > 0, and the
%                pairwise resultants. cad_lift derives the level-below family
%                instead by iterating "which polynomials were read" to a
%                fixpoint, and answering that is the 3^|F| enumeration; the
%                projection computes a sufficient family once, in time
%                polynomial in the family. OPERATOR ONLY: the delineability
%                argument that makes projection + lifting a correct CAD is
%                not claimed here
%   ---- THE RUN COMPUTES A CAD (GAP_PLAN.md Tier 1, 2026-09-29) ----
%   cad_roots   the roots over a point as a sorted list: rootat? (zeros of the
%                members not identically zero on the fibre), rl? (L lists them,
%                strictly increasing; unique, rl_unique), rtl, nroot, rt.
%                Wherever the walk's certificate holds the list exists and is
%                the walk's gap roots over any passing separators (rtl_groots)
%   cad_fibre   one fibre: the stack index sidx (twice the roots below, plus one
%                at a root), one point per band (bpts), the sign vectors at the
%                roots (rsvl) and on the bands (bsvl); the sign vector at a
%                point is the entry its index names (sidx_root, sidx_band)
%   cad_gpar    the walk's root lists at two points with the same gap pattern
%                run in parallel: same length, same count below each
%                separator, every band reached at a separator, same sign
%                vectors, roots within eta (gr_near, from mroot_near)
%   cad_local   near a certified point, with the reads agreeing, the stack is
%                the same (loc_stack) and the roots move continuously
%                (loc_cont) -- from near_cells and mroot_near
%   cad_conn    connected sets (every locally constant property is constant)
%                and continuity on a set; a segment of a line is connected
%                (conn_line, from loc_const)
%   cad_stack   DELINEABILITY (sign-invariant sense): delin_cl? (roots listed, constant
%                count, continuous root functions, every member sign-invariant
%                on every section and band).  stack_const: if the walk's
%                certificate holds at a point p0 of a connected set and its
%                reads at p0 keep their signs across the set, the family is
%                delineable over it (delin_cl?) -- the run-time check becomes
%                delineability
%   cad_slab    a slab over a connected set is connected when its slices are
%                intervals and a continuous section runs through them
%   cad_stkc    every cell of a delineable stack is such a slab, hence
%                connected (conn_stk), and the family has one sign vector on
%                it (stk_sinv)
%   cad_tower   a certified tower over a connected base cell is a CAD
%                (tower_cad: cad_over?, every level delineable over every cell
%                below it, every cell connected); its cells tcell partition the
%                cylinder (tcell_cover, tcell_disj) and the top family is
%                sign-invariant on each (top_sinv)
%   cad_run     THE n-LEVEL RUN COMPUTES A CAD ADAPTED TO F: when decn_o's
%                certificate holds, the cells rcell(s, A) (outer sector s,
%                stack indices A) cover R^(k+1), are pairwise disjoint and
%                connected, are the sections and bands of continuous root
%                functions at every level, and every polynomial of F has one
%                sign on each (cad_run); the outer sectors partition the line
%                (sects_disj; sect_inv.sects_cover).  Every cell, at every
%                level, is first-order definable (rcf_cells.rcell_fod_le; see
%                cad_verified)
%   cad_out     THE CAD AS DATA (executable): per sector, the tree of the
%                walk's cells (kids: separators and root gaps with their stack
%                indices; ctree), one record per path -- sector, stack
%                indices, exact sample descriptor, F's sign vector read by the
%                descriptor's own oracle
%   cad_out_ok  the records are the CAD of cad_run: every record's sample lies
%                in the cell its sector and indices name, with F's sign vector
%                there, and every nonempty cell has a record (cad_out_ok; one
%                level: kids_lvl, the children carry the stack indices of
%                their points and every index up to 2 nroot occurs)
%   cad_fold    deciding from the returned CAD (executable): the outer
%                quantifier over the sectors, each inner one over the stack
%                indices of the next level, Psi on the recorded sign vector
%   cad_fold_ok ONE CAD DECIDES EVERY SENTENCE OVER F: a cylindrical
%                decomposition (cad_over?) on whose top cells F is
%                sign-invariant (fsinv?), given by sample records (recin?,
%                reccov?), decides every formula over F that quantifies over
%                its levels, at any point of the base (afold_sem, a theorem
%                about any such tower); so the
%                decomposition cad_out computes once decides every sentence with the same
%                polynomials and variables, whatever its quantifiers and its
%                Boolean combination of sign conditions (cad_decides)
%   cad_sample  sample points: each record's sample is a point of R^(k+1)
%                with the recorded signs (rec_pt); a sign condition holds
%                somewhere iff at some record (cad_sat); the fold over a sector
%                is the meaning of the rest of the sentence (all but the outer
%                quantifier) with the outer variable at the sector's sample
%                (cad_outer); for every F and at least two quantifiers some u
%                gives such a decomposition (cad_complete)
%   cad_out_ex  the unit circle: certificate at u = 0, the 13 cells in 15
%                records with their stack indices and sign vectors,
%                cad_out_ok for that F, and cad_found stopping at u = 0
%   rcf_fol     first-order formulas over the reals with polynomial sign
%                atoms (Fm, fsem; variables are positions in the point list);
%                a dummy variable at any depth (mins, meval_mins; shiftF,
%                fsem_shift); fod?(n)(S): S is first-order definable in R^n
%   rcf_cad_def the formulas of the cells (executable): rootF (a root over
%                the point), geF (at least i roots below), sidxF (the stack
%                index), stkF / cellF (stack and tower cells), cmpF / sectF
%                (the sectors of the line)
%   root_fol    rootF, geF and sidxF mean what they say at every point
%                (rootF_ok, geF_ok, sidxF_ok -- the root lists exist
%                everywhere, rl_all); stkF defines the stack cell (stkF_ok)
%   rcf_cells   EVERY CELL OF THE RUN, AT EVERY LEVEL, IS FIRST-ORDER
%                DEFINABLE (rcell_ok, rcell_fod; rcell_ok_le, rcell_fod_le for
%                the lower levels), with no certificate needed; the sectors
%                (sectF_ok, through each algebraic number's closed isolating
%                interval) and tower cells over a definable base (cellF_ok);
%                the graph of a root function over a definable cell is
%                definable (sec_graph, graph_fod; applied to the run in
%                cad_verified).  Definable sets are also quantifier-free
%                definable (qelim_ok's fod_qfd, Tarski-Seidenberg), so
%                semi-algebraic, indeed defined over Q (cad_sa)
%   cad_verified THE RUN COMPUTES A CAD ADAPTED TO F (BPR Def. 5.1, semi-
%                algebraic in the sense of first-order definable; the
%                quantifier-free sense follows by qelim_ok's fod_qfd and is
%                stated for these cells as cad_sa's cad_cells_sa), RETURNED AS DATA: cad_of? -- the cells of R^(k+1) cover it and are
%                disjoint; the cells of every level are connected and
%                first-order definable; cylindrical over delineable families
%                whose root functions have definable graphs; F sign-invariant
%                on each cell of R^(k+1); and cad_out lists every nonempty
%                cell at least once, and nothing else, with samples and sign
%                vectors; cad_verified (under decn_o's certificate) and
%                cad_exists (for every F in at least two variables, some u)
%   cad_found_def THE COMPLETE CAD ALGORITHM (executable): cad_found raises
%                the search-effort parameter u from 0 until the run's
%                certificate holds (as decn_u does for the decision) and
%                returns that u with the CAD's records (cad_out)
%   cad_found_ok cad_found always returns a CAD: for two or more variables
%                its result satisfies cad_of? (cad_found_cad) and its records
%                decide every sentence over F in the same variables
%                (cad_found_decides)
%   cad_decide7_def the decision of (cad :cad-only? t) in a theory that does not
%                import cad_decide8 (decide8 otherwise) (executable): decide7 =
%                decn_u (the verified CAD engine) for two or more quantifiers,
%                decq2 (the sectors of the line) for one quantifier or none;
%                no witness search
%                and no two-variable fast path
%   cad_decide7 decide7 is correct and complete (decide7_correct,
%                decide7_decides)
%
% Quantifier elimination (GAP_PLAN.md Tier 2)
%   qe_ldd       duplicate removal on a list (executable); qe_ldd_ok: same
%                members (ldd_mem)
%   qe_fm_def    formulas for QE (executable): qf? (no quantifier), f_orl /
%                f_andl (n-ary disjunction / conjunction), qfsem (rcf_fol's
%                fsem under its own name); qe_fm: their meaning
%   qe_def       QE FOR ONE FREE VARIABLE (executable): qe1_o runs decn_o's
%                first level for the bound quantifiers below one free
%                variable; each sector of the line gets the value of the
%                bound part at its sample and the projection set's signs;
%                when sectors with the same signs on a chosen index set agree
%                (sep1?), the answer is the disjunction of those sign
%                conditions, otherwise of the sectors' descriptions sectX
%                (qe_ep_def); qe1_u raises u, qe1 takes a Boolean combination
%   qe_ep_def    QUANTIFIER-FREE COMPARISON WITH A REAL ALGEBRAIC NUMBER
%                (executable): an EP record (a rational value, or a
%                polynomial with the number as its one root on an interval
%                where it changes sign) checked at run time (epok?); ltQ, eqQ,
%                gtQ (x <, =, > the number), cmpX, sectX (x in a sector),
%                quantifier-free when the check passes; epc builds the record
%   qe_ep        they are correct (ep_side: the sign of the polynomial on the
%                interval tells the side; ltQ_ok, eqQ_ok, gtQ_ok, cmpX_ok,
%                sectX_ok)
%   qe_ep_ok     the check always passes (epc_ok): the derivative of the
%                order below the multiplicity has a simple root, which is a
%                sign change (simple_change, from NASALib's extremum lemmas)
%   qe_mrg_def   MERGED ANSWERS IN ONE FREE VARIABLE (executable,
%                2026-09-30): a run of consecutive TRUE sectors is one
%                interval -- not below the start of its first sector (lowX),
%                not above the end of its last (uppX); mrgX is the disjunction
%                over the maximal runs (a one-sector run is its sectX);
%                qe_def's qe1_sm uses it when it has fewer sign atoms (natoms)
%   qe_mrg       mrgX is right: the sectors of a sorted root list form a
%                chain of neighbours (sects_chain), and mrgX holds at x iff x
%                is in a TRUE sector (mrgX_sem); quantifier-free (qf_mrgX)
%   qe1_ok       qe1 is correct: qe1_correct (under the run's certificate the
%                output holds at x iff the bound quantifiers do), qe1_qf (qf
%                means quantifier-free), qe1_u_ok (the escalation always
%                reaches the certificate), qe1_bf ((cad-qe) proves from
%                qe8_correct or qe_correct)
%   qe1_full     QE FOR ONE FREE VARIABLE IS COMPLETE: qe1 always returns a
%                quantifier-free formula equivalent to the input (qe1_complete)
%   qe2_def      QE FOR SEVERAL FREE VARIABLES (executable): qe2_c runs
%                decn_o on the free and bound variables together; over each
%                sector s of the outermost free variable the free cells are
%                the records' first m - 1 stack indices, a cell's value is the
%                fold of the bound quantifiers over its records, its signature
%                the signs of the free levels' polynomials at a record's
%                sample; secout(s) is the disjunction of the TRUE signatures'
%                sign conditions when equal signatures mean equal values
%                (sep2?), otherwise of the TRUE cells' formulas; the answer is
%                the disjunction of sectX(s) AND secout(s); qe2_u raises u
%   qe2_ok       qe2 is correct: qe2_correct (under the run's certificate the
%                output holds at (y1, ..., ym) iff the bound quantifiers do),
%                qe2_qf, qe2_u_ok, qe2_bf
%   qe_thom      2C, ONE FIBER: a derivative-closed family (dcl?: lderiv of
%                every member is a member) keeps each member's sign between
%                two points with the same sign vector (fiber_const, Thom's
%                lemma at the fiber polynomial's actual degree), so no root
%                lies between them and the stack index is the same
%                (fiber_sidx)
%   qe_tsep      2C, THE TOWER: in a CAD with derivative-closed families two
%                cells whose points have the same sign vector on every family
%                are the same cell (stk_sep, tcell_sep)
%   qe2_sep      2C, THE ENTRIES: if a sector's free families are
%                derivative-closed, its free cells' signatures separate
%                (ents_sep: sep2? holds, so the sector's answer is
%                quantifier-free)
%   runT_def     the n-level run from a GIVEN initial tower (executable):
%                runqT, runsectsT, runtwT, runokT (decn_o's certificate),
%                cad_outT (cad_out's records)
%   runT_ok      under runokT each sector's tower is a CAD with records, for
%                any initial tower (sectorT_cad, cad_outT_in, cad_outT_cov,
%                sectorT_fsinv, sectorT_recs)
%   qe2c_def     2C, QE FROM A DERIVATIVE-CLOSED RUN (executable): the initial
%                tower's free families grow by the derivatives of every
%                sector's closed free families until a round adds nothing
%                (augT); dclok checks the closure; qe2c_o answers from it
%   qe2c_ok      qe2c_correct: when qe2c_o succeeds, its answer is
%                quantifier-free and holds exactly where the formula does
%   aug_univ     the augmentation stays inside a finite universe closed under
%                the engine's reads and, at the free levels, under lderiv
%                (tinvd?, treads_subd, zipadd_invd, taug_invd), so it stops
%                (aug_stop, aug_same)
%   aug_ev       for every large u the augmented run succeeds (qe2c_ev,
%                qe2c_complete): the certificate from any initial tower
%                (runokT_ev), the rounds settle (aug1_evc, augT_evc), and a
%                round that adds nothing leaves the free families
%                derivative-closed (fix_dclok)
%   qe2c_u_def   qe2c: the derivative-closed QE with u raised until it
%                succeeds (executable)
%   qe2c_full    qe2c always succeeds and is quantifier-free (qe2c_u_ok,
%                qe2c_isqf, qe2c_bf)
%   qe_all_def   qe(m, os, F, phi): qe1 for one free variable; for more, qe2
%                when its answer is quantifier-free, otherwise qe2c
%   qe_all       QUANTIFIER ELIMINATION FOR THE REALS, COMPUTED AND PROVED:
%                qe_correct (qe's output holds at a point iff the quantified
%                formula does), qe_isqf (always quantifier-free), qe_complete
%                (for every m >= 1: a quantifier-free equivalent) --
%                Tarski-Seidenberg
%   qe_cad_ex    (cad-qe) examples: a window of times, square roots, a
%                discriminant, two bound variables (x^2 + y^2 < r iff r > 0),
%                two free variables (t = a AND t = b; the quadratic's
%                discriminant a^2 >= 4 b), an alternating prefix, grouped
%                binders
%   ---- Collins' projection (COLLINS_PLAN.md) ------------------------------
%   rdet_nl      ring_det's rdet is NASALib's det (rdet_nl), so NASALib's
%                matrix theory applies: the transpose (rdet_tr), two equal
%                rows or columns give 0 (rdet_rows_eq, rdet_cols_eq), and the
%                left kernel in both directions -- rdet /= 0 kills every
%                left-kernel vector (rdet_lker: an inverse exists), rdet = 0
%                gives a nonzero one (rdet_zero_lker: the zero row of
%                Gaussian elimination, matrix_diag)
%   rdet_lin     the determinant is linear in each column (rdet_col_lin) and
%                is the v-weighted combination of the determinants with unit
%                columns (rdet_col_sum)
%   cpoly_alg    complex polynomials as functions with coefficient witnesses:
%                cpol? (degree at most n), clead? (degree exactly n, leading
%                coefficient c); products through croots' factorization
%                (clead_cprod, cprod_lead, clead_mul), so no convolution
%                formula; zero or exact degree (cpol_cases), uniqueness
%                (clead_deg), no zero divisors (cmul_zero), small multiples
%                vanish (clow_zero), the factor theorem (cfactor), linear
%                cancellation (ccancel); complex ring identities by the cring
%                strategy (components)
%   cpoly_div    divisibility (cdvd?) and common monic divisors (ccd?: k
%                linear factors dividing both) -- the gcd degree without the
%                gcd: ccd_0, ccd_down, cdvd_cof (cofactor degree), ccd_up
%                (a common root of the cofactors), ccoprime (G | U F, no
%                common root of G and F: G | U)
%   sres_rows    the rows of the subresultant matrix sylj are shifted
%                coefficient lists: a complex row combination c is the
%                coefficient list of u*A + v*B (comb_poly), so c is in the
%                left kernel of the square part iff u*A + v*B has degree below
%                j (comb_low, comb_zero); complex kernels meet rdet_nl through
%                real and imaginary parts (rdet_ckernel, rdet_zero_ckernel)
%   sres_thm     THE SUBRESULTANT THEOREM (sres_ccd): for real F, G of exact
%                degrees p, q and k <= min(p, q), F and G have a common
%                divisor of degree k iff psc_0, ..., psc_(k-1) vanish; the
%                halves sres_zero (a common divisor gives the kernel vector
%                (G/h, -F/h)) and sres_nz (a kernel vector at the exact gcd
%                degree contradicts coprime cofactors, ccoprime)
%   sres_gcd     the k-th subresultant polynomial Sk (coefficients: the
%                determinant with the last column at each degree) is u*F + v*G
%                (sres_comb); common roots are its roots (sres_common) and at
%                the exact gcd degree every root of Sk is a common root
%                (sres_roots)
%   sres_eval    subres' psc and subres2's sresc at a point are these
%                determinants (psc_ev, sresc_ev)
%   cpoly_der    the formal derivative (cder?, unique by padding the
%                coefficient witness: cder_unique); the product rule with a
%                linear factor (cder_lin) and with a power of one (cder_pow)
%   cpoly_ord    the order of a root: P = (z - y)^m Q, Q(y) /= 0 (ordp?);
%                existence (ord_ex), uniqueness (ord_unique, from pw_cancel),
%                orders add under products (ord_mul), a divisor has smaller
%                order (ord_dvd), the derivative drops the order by one
%                (ord_der); ord(P, y) as a function (ord_def, ord_eq)
%   cpoly_dist   the number of distinct roots: for F of degree e whose
%                distinct roots are listed by L, F and F' have a common
%                divisor of degree e - length(L) (dist_lo) and none of degree
%                e - length(L) + 1 (dist_hi) -- so the psc of (f, f') counts
%                distinct complex roots (with sres_thm)
%   croot_lsc    roots of a real polynomial of fixed degree move continuously
%                with its coefficients: rp_near (each new root is near an old
%                one, croot_near), rp_lsc (each old root has a new one near
%                it: cprod_far against cpoly_diff_bound); the distinct roots
%                form a list (rp_list), conjugates of roots are roots
%                (rp_conj), rp_cder (the derivative), dist_same (equal psc
%                patterns of (f, f') give equally many distinct roots)
%   croot_disc   one root per disc (pigeonhole): an eps-separated distinct
%                list covered by a list no longer than itself has exactly one
%                cover point in each eps-disc (cover_len, disc_one)
%   ev_near      "eventually near p1 in C" and "for every small eps",
%                closed under conjunction and bounded quantification (ev_all,
%                sm_all); mpoly values are eventually close (ev_cont)
%   col_def      Collins' projection hypothesis colh?(G, C): on C the kept
%                reducta of each member (N-reducta rule, kept?) have
%                sign-invariant leading coefficients and psc_j(f, f'), and
%                kept reducta of two members sign-invariant psc_j(f, h)
%   col_eff      the effective degree edeg is the same at every point of C
%                (col_edeg); a member over a point is the real polynomial of
%                its reductum (mev_rdc, mev_rp) or identically zero; the
%                projection's psc have one zero pattern on C (col_psc1/2)
%   col_real     abstractly: if over p the roots move continuously (up?,
%                lo?), one per disc (one?), common roots stay common (sh?)
%                and the roots over p1 are 2 eps apart (sp?), then the real
%                roots over p are bt of those over p1, in order, with the
%                same members vanishing (bt_list, bt_van, bt_mono)
%   col_pair     these hypotheses hold near p1 in C (ff_hyp): rp_near /
%                rp_lsc on coefficients, dist_same + disc_one, and for common
%                roots S_kappa of sres_gcd (sh_mid1) or the smaller member
%                dividing the other (sh_full1/2)
%   col_loc      THE LOCAL COLLINS LEMMA collins_loc: near each p1 in C the
%                stack keeps its sign vectors (rsvl, bsvl) and root count, and
%                the roots move by less than any eta
%   col_stack    DELINEABILITY FROM COLLINS' PROJECTION collins_stack: C
%                connected and colh?(G, C) imply delin_cl?(G, C); kcol_stack:
%                the same from col_pair's kconst? (constant effective degrees
%                and common-divisor degrees, what the proof uses; colh? gives
%                it, colh_kconst)
%   cad_projn_def  Collins' N-reducta projection, executable: kd (kept
%                degrees, stopping after a coefficient that normalizes to a
%                nonzero constant), pj1/pj2/pj3 (coefficients, psc with the
%                derivative, psc of pairs), projn (normalized, no constants,
%                no repetitions)
%   cad_projn    projn_ok: sign-invariance of projn(G) on C gives colh?(G, C)
%   cad_projb_def  a smaller projection for the bottom step (PROJ_PLAN.md
%                P2), executable: per member its top coefficient that does
%                not normalize to 0 and the first psc with its derivative
%                that does not, per pair the first psc that does not (projb;
%                pdownb is its checked basis as a family, fsplit)
%   cad_projb    kconst_nz / projb_kconst: where every member of projb(G) is
%                nonzero, kconst?(G, C) holds (the first psc that is nonzero
%                fixes the common-divisor degree, ccd1_iff / ccd2_iff);
%                kconst_one on a point; projb_delin: delineability
%   mpoly_gcd_def  gcd (subresultant remainder sequences, NEXT_PLAN.md P5;
%                primitive ones in P3), contents, Yun's squarefree
%                decomposition and a pairwise coprime basis of multivariate
%                polynomials (basis, exps), executable and untrusted: only
%                results that pass mpoly_cert_def's check are used
%                (PROJ_PLAN.md P3)
%   mpoly_cert_def  fsplit(U): the proposed basis of U when every member is
%                c * product of b^e over it by normal forms (ucheck?), else
%                U; executable
%   mpoly_cert   fsplit_cinv / fsplit_nz: one sign on C (nonzero on C) for
%                every member of fsplit(U) gives the same for every member of
%                U (ucert_eval, pprod_cinv, pprod_nz)
%   col_tower_def  the Collins tower, executable: ctw(F, k) is F's k - 1
%                projections and F, lowest first (pdown = the checked basis of
%                projn as a family, fsplit); pbot(F, k) is the k-th
%                projection
%   col_tower    ctw_cad: on a connected C where pbot(F, k) is sign-invariant
%                the Collins tower is a CAD (cad_over?): every level is
%                delineable over every stack cell, by collins_stack and
%                projn_ok (ptower_cad; fam_cinv through fsplit_cinv)
%   col_towerb   ptower_cad2 / ctw_cad2: the tower is a CAD over a connected
%                C where its lowest family is delineable; bot_cad (projb of
%                the lowest family nonzero on C), bot_cad1 (C one point)
%   decc_def     the decision over the Collins tower, executable: decc_o is
%                decn_o's walk without the read closures, cut at the roots of
%                pbot(F, k); its certificate okw_o is okn_o without the
%                closure condition
%   col_sem      the truth of the inner quantifiers is the same at all points
%                of a cell over which the tower is a CAD (sem_cad); a
%                root-free interval stays in one stack cell (rf_sidx), so the
%                fold of the walk's cell values is the quantifier (rfc_cad,
%                sem_cvlc); the outer family is sign-invariant on its sectors
%                (sinv_sect)
%   decc_walk    innern_cad: over a base cell where the tower is a CAD the
%                walk reads the truth of the inner quantifiers (innern_sem's
%                induction with the CAD in place of the closure); okw_ev:
%                its certificate holds for every large u
%   decc_ok      THE COLLINS DECISION IS CORRECT AND COMPLETE: decc_correct
%                (a certified value is the truth of the sentence) and
%                decc_complete (some u certifies, two quantifiers or more)
%   col_run      THE COLLINS PROJECTIONS FORM A CAD (col_cad_run), with no
%                certificate: for every F and k >= 1 the cells over the
%                sectors of pbot(F, k) cover R^(k+1), are disjoint,
%                connected and first-order definable at every level, the
%                tower is a CAD over each sector, the root graphs are
%                definable, and F has one sign vector on every cell
%   col_out      the Collins run's records (cad_outc), executable
%   col_out_ok   the records are the cells: ctree_inw / ctree_coverw (the
%                tree under the walk's certificate okw_o), cad_outc_ok
%   col_verified ccad_of?: cad_of? (BPR Def. 5.1 + data) for the Collins
%                run; col_verified (holds when decc_o certifies) and
%                col_exists (for some u)
%   det_fast_def the determinant with every minor computed once (detf, n 2^n
%                products instead of n!), the minors looked up in a trie of
%                their column sets (DTrie, dbits, tbuild; PROJ_PLAN.md P1),
%                executable; detf0 is the former table-scan version
%   det_fast     detf_det: detf(n, M) = det(n, M); the trie answers what the
%                table did (tlook_tins, dbits_inj, tlook_tbuild, dtabt_dtab,
%                detf_eq); bareiss_def's bdet falls back to detf
%   mpoly_dom    no zero divisors: when p is not the zero function and p * q
%                is, q is (cancel, cancel_pow), by induction on the sizes,
%                through NASALib's univariate polynomials (poly_nodiv)
%   bareiss_def  the determinant by fraction-free elimination (Bareiss, O(n^3)
%                instead of n 2^n): bdet, with each exact division by mex
%                checked by normal forms, a zero pivot replaced by adding the
%                first row whose entry is not zero, detf when a check fails;
%                executable (NEXT_PLAN.md P5)
%   bareiss      bdet_det: mnorm(bdet(n, M)) = mnorm(det(n, M)); the block
%                invariant bar_inv (value * p^(m-1) = det) by Chio's identity
%                from column operations (chio_id), the checked divisions
%                (elim_val) and mpoly_dom's cancellation; the projection's
%                psc uses bdet (cad_projn_def's pscg, cad_projn's pscg_eq)
%   decc_u_def   decc_u: decc_o from u = 0 until its certificate holds
%   decc_u       decc_u_correct, decc_u_complete
%   decb_def     decb_o: decc_o with the line cut at the roots of the smaller
%                bottom-step projection (cbotb) when every member is nonzero
%                at every non-root sector's rational sample (nzb?), else
%                decc_o; executable
%   decb_ok      decb_correct, decb_complete: on each base cell the tower is
%                a CAD (sect_nz, fsplit_nz, sect_cadb), then decc_ok's
%                argument
%   decb_u_def   decb_u: decb_o from u = 0 until its certificate holds
%   decb_u       decb_u_correct, decb_u_complete
%   decb_run     DECIDE8'S RUN IS A CAD (decb_cad_run), with no certificate:
%                col_run's conditions for the sectors decb_o cuts the line
%                into (bsects: those of the smaller bottom step when nzb?
%                holds, else those of pbot(F, k)); decb_cad_input: the input
%                family has one sign vector on every cell of the run over its
%                checked basis (svec_smap)
%   fsplit_top_def  the input family over its checked basis, executable:
%                ubs (the members as polynomials), sce (a member's cofactor
%                sign and exponents), smap (the members' sign vector from the
%                basis'), spsi (the matrix read through smap)
%   fsplit_top   sem_split: when every member passes the check, the sentence
%                over fam(B) and spsi means what it means over F and Psi
%                (svec_smap, sce_sign, sprd_sign, spw_sign)
%   cad_decide8_def  decide8: decb_u for two or more quantifiers, over the
%                checked basis of the input family when every member passes
%                (fsplit_top_def), decq2 for one -- what (cad) uses in a
%                theory importing cad_decide8
%   cad_decide8  decide8_correct, decide8_decides (sound and complete)
%   cad_decide8_ex  (cad) and (cad-direct) through decide8: circle, k3_lin,
%                l3_quad (the last > 600 s with the read-closure decision);
%                (cad :cad-only? t) with one quantifier and on a hypothesis
%   qe1c_def     quantifier elimination for one free variable over the
%                Collins run (qe1cc: qe1_sm on the Collins sectors), executable
%   qe1c_ok      qe1cc_correct, qe1cc_bf: always answers, quantifier-free,
%                equivalent
%   qe2cc_def    m >= 2 free variables over the Collins run (qe2cc: secout on
%                the records of cad_outc), executable
%   qe2cc_ok     csector_recs, csect_sem, qe2cc_correct, qe2cc_bf: equivalent
%                (quantifier-free when its flag says so)
%   ctwd_def     ctwd(F, k, d): the Collins tower with the lowest d families
%                closed under the derivative (dch, dclf), executable
%   ctwd         pstower_cad (a chain whose families CONTAIN the projection of
%                the next is a CAD), ctwd_cad, ctwd_dcl (free levels closed
%                under the derivative)
%   twrun_def    the run over any tower: certificate twok, records cad_outw
%   twrun_ok     twok_ev, wsector_recs (records in and covering the cells of
%                every sector of a tower that is a CAD over its sectors)
%   qe2cd_def    m >= 2 free variables over ctwd (qe2cd), executable
%   qe2cd_ok     qe2cd_bf: always answers, always quantifier-free, equivalent
%                (Thom separation on the derivative-closed free levels)
%   qe8_def      qe8: qe1cc for one free variable; qe2cc for m >= 2 when its
%                answer is quantifier-free, else qe2cd -- every branch the
%                Collins run; what (cad-qe) uses in a theory that imports qe8
%   qe8          qe8_correct, qe8_complete: always a quantifier-free equivalent
%   qe8_ex       (cad-qe) through qe8: qe_cad_ex's six equivalences and an
%                alternating prefix
%
% The Collins CAD in every dimension, found, and semi-algebraic (after M-K):
%   col_line     ccells?(k, F): col_cad_run's conditions; col_cad_line (one
%                variable: the sectors of the roots of F), col_cells (every
%                k: the Collins CAD in every number of variables)
%   col_found_def  col_found: the Collins CAD with the search for u built in
%                (one variable: lrecs, a record per sector), executable
%   col_found_ok col_found_cad (always the CAD as data, every number of
%                variables), col_found_ccad (ccad_of? at the u found),
%                cfound_decides / col_found_decides (its records decide every
%                sentence over F)
%   qelim_def    qelim(phi, m): quantifier elimination for every first-order
%                formula, from the inside out (qe8 for one existential, decide8
%                for sentences); encF / encB encode a quantifier-free formula
%                for the prenex procedures, executable
%   qelim_ok     enc_ok, qelim_qf, qelim_ok; fod_qfd: first-order definable =
%                quantifier-free definable (Tarski-Seidenberg, for formulas
%                with rational coefficients and no real parameters)
%   cad_sa       col_cells_sa, cad_cells_sa: every cell of both CADs, at every
%                level, is semi-algebraic in the quantifier-free sense, indeed
%                defined over Q (and so is every root graph of the Collins CAD)
%
% Formulas as people write them (FORMS_PLAN.md):
%   gform_def    Gm: formulas with PVS's own connectives (gsem), g2f into
%                rcf_fol, decide_g (qelim on the Collins run), qe_g; executable
%   gform_ok     g2f_ok, decide_g_ok (decide_g IFF gsem at null: what (cad)
%                instantiates for a formula of any shape), qe_g_qf, qe_g_ok
%                (what (cad-qe) instantiates)
%   pvs_cad      everything (cad) and (cad-qe) need in one import (cad_decide8,
%                qe8, gform_ok, mpoly_embed, cad_endgame); the CAD theorems
%                of col_line, col_found_ok, cad_verified, cad_found_ok,
%                cad_sa, qe_all and complete_all are outside its import
%                closure (all are imported here)
%   trans_bounds polynomial bounds of sin, cos, exp, ln and atan in the form
%                (cad) reads, from NASALib's trig and lnexp lemmas: the
%                ranges, bounds of degree 3 (t - t^3/6 <= sin(t) <= t for
%                t >= 0, 1 + t <= exp(t), ln(t) <= t - 1 for t > 0, ...)
%                and of higher degree (sin to 7, cos to 6, from NASALib's
%                sin_bounds and cos_bounds) (NEXT_PLAN.md T5)
%   pvs_cad_num  pvs_cad with NASALib's interval_arith@strategies and
%                trans_bounds: (cad) also encloses constants such as pi, e,
%                sin(1) between rationals (TERMS_PLAN.md T2), and bounds and
%                encloses sin, cos, exp, ln and atan of terms with constants
%                of the sequent (NEXT_PLAN.md T5)
%   cad_showcase (cad) and (cad-qe) on 43 examples: sentences, (cad) on
%                chosen formulas of a sequent ((cad n), (cad (m n)), (cad +),
%                (cad -), (cad *)), the CAD engine alone, elimination
%   cad_forms_ex regression: every connective on both sides of the sequent,
%                NOT over compound formulas, the connectives' synonyms,
%                binders over the five subtypes, unknowns with the bounds
%                their types give, TRUE / FALSE and comparisons of a term with
%                itself beside quantified parts, (cad *) and (cad-qe) on such
%                formulas, (cad-direct) on a general formula, (cad-qe) on an
%                alternating prefix (79 lemmas)
%   cad_terms_ex regression for (cad) on real terms beyond polynomials
%                (TERMS_PLAN.md T2-T4): constants that interval arithmetic
%                evaluates (pi, e, sin(1), ln(2), atan(1), two at once, one
%                in a hypothesis) by enclosures, sqrt, abs, max, min and
%                quotients by their exact facts, also in formulas that are
%                not prenex, and sqrt, abs, max, min and quotients under
%                binders, in any order and nesting, read into formulas
%                without them; sin, cos, exp, ln and atan of terms with
%                constants (NEXT_PLAN.md T5: bounds, enclosures over pieces);
%                (cad-num) and (cad-facts) called directly, with options; a
%                failing (cad) that leaves the sequent as it was (96 lemmas);
%                imports pvs_cad_num
%   cad_decide7_ex  (cad :cad-only? t) through decide7 in a theory that
%                imports neither cad_decide8 nor gform_ok
%   cad_diag_ex  the measuring and inspection commands (cad-showf, cad-bench,
%                cad-mn, cad-mx, cad-time, cad-m3, cad-qeshow), each run once
%                before (cad) or (cad-qe) closes the goal
%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
